"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkgrc||=[]).push([[98188],{92442(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/11/13/clio-in-2026-agent-to-marketplace","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/clio-in-2026-agent-to-marketplace","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-clio-in-2026-agent-to-marketplace.mdx","title":"CLIO in 2026: from agent to marketplace","description":"In 2026, CLIO, the agent layer of IOWarp, grew from a runtime and a tool kit into a science agent, a desktop app, a coding agent and a marketplace of agents.","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"software-drop","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/software-drop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"Scientific data management","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/scientific-data-management"},{"inline":true,"label":"NSF","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/nsf"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":5.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"CLIO in 2026: from agent to marketplace","description":"In 2026, CLIO, the agent layer of IOWarp, grew from a runtime and a tool kit into a science agent, a desktop app, a coding agent and a marketplace of agents.","authors":"grc","tags":["software-drop","Release","AI agents","Scientific data management","NSF","IOWarp"],"image":"/img/projects/clio/clio-in-2026-og.png"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"GRC at SC26: a visitor\'s guide","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/grc-at-sc26"}},"content":"CLIO is the agent layer of [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/), GRC\'s platform for managing scientific data and context for AI. In February 2026 it had two parts, a storage runtime and a tool kit. By November 13 it had six, each with its own release schedule, and together they had published more than 300 tagged releases on GitHub this year. This is a tour of what each part does and how it got there.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src=\\"/img/software/clio-agent/mark.svg\\" width=\\"160\\" height=\\"160\\" alt=\\"The CLIO Agent owl\\" />\\n  <figcaption>The CLIO Agent owl. Core and Coder use distinct product marks; Kit uses the IOWarp mark shown on its own site.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## The base: CLIO Core and CLIO Kit\\n\\nCLIO Core provides the storage and runtime services underneath everything else: it keeps data and context on distributed, tiered storage close to where agents and applications need it. Core shipped 24 releases between version 0.6.5 on February 23 and version 2.4.1 on November 11. Version 2.3.0, released October 9, brought the GPU runtime up on Intel GPUs, raising the number of passing tests on Argonne\'s Aurora system from 104 to 254. The same release served HDF5 reads from the CLIO cache tier, cut the latency of a cross-node collective operation by 3.1 times, and fixed seven crash-consistency bugs found across five fault scenarios. [Luke Logan](/center/team/luke-logan/) leads Core development.\\n\\nCLIO Kit connects agents to scientific and HPC tools through Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers. It went from version 2.0.1 in February to 2.12.7 in November, about 60 releases in all. [Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/) and [Isa Muradli](/center/team/isa-muradli/) are its main contributors.\\n\\n## An agent for science: CLIO Agent and its desktop\\n\\nCLIO Agent made its first 2026 release, version 0.3.1, on April 27, and reached version 0.9.7 on November 13 after nearly 90 releases. Version 0.9.0 in July added three ways to control what the agent does: a planning mode in which the agent may write only its plan until a person approves it, project hooks that run a lab\'s own scripts before or after each action, and sessions that loop, pursue a stated goal or run on a schedule. All three go through one permission system, where a deny rule always beats an allow.\\n\\nVersion 0.9.7 builds web and scholarly search into the agent. Its literature tools query OpenAlex, Crossref, arXiv, Europe PMC, OSTI.gov, DataCite and OpenCitations, and ask before the first query to each source. Deep research now delivers its report with full references, and the agent can see the plots and page renders its own scripts open. Long conversions and builds run until they finish instead of hitting a fixed time limit.\\n\\nPeople reach the agent through GACT TUI, a terminal and desktop front end that can drive any compliant agent back end. It went from version 0.2.1 in April to 0.13.0 on November 13, about 60 releases. Version 0.13 adds Spanish, Japanese and Greek to the interface, marked as machine translations until native speakers review them, along with a guided tour for new users. Cernuda leads the agent and its front end.\\n\\n## A marketplace of science agents\\n\\nThe CLIO Agent Marketplace holds the agents and shared capabilities that CLIO Agent loads. Its first release, version 0.5.2 on June 17, shipped agents that analyze EarthScope GNSS station data through the [National Data Platform](/gnosis/articles/2024/11/01/utah-ndp-partnership); they passed all seven tasks of the team\'s test matrix with three different open-weight models.\\n\\nVersion 0.7.0, released October 28, replaced that first layout with agent classes and capability plugins that agents can combine. It introduced CLIO Data, which inspects, checks and converts HDF5, netCDF-4, ADIOS2 and Parquet data and keeps its conversion checks with its procedures. Its deep-research guidance records verified claims, rejected claims and open gaps separately, so a report shows which evidence still needs checking. Version 0.8.0 followed on November 13, the twelfth marketplace release of the year.\\n\\n## CLIO Coder\\n\\nCLIO Coder is an open-source coding agent for scientific software and everyday engineering. The experimental first release on April 24 already ran worker processes for batch tasks, kept a record of every run and gated tool access through three safety modes. About 60 releases later, version 0.8.0 on November 13 targets people who run a coding agent beside colleagues, other agents and scripts, such as researchers on a shared login node. Two sessions can share a folder safely, the sandbox hides credentials and token files from contained commands, and headless runs end with one machine-readable result for automation. A per-project store keeps results and datasets outside the working tree, and diagnostic reports are shown to the user before anything leaves the machine. A new guide covers installing it on HPC login nodes. [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) leads its development.\\n\\n## Around the stack\\n\\nTwo releases outside the CLIO repositories support the same work. Jarvis-CD 1.8, released August 6 and maintained by Logan, ships execution profiles for scientific applications, services and I/O interceptors that the maintainers keep current, the same ones used in a cross-cluster evaluation campaign. It also reconciles Slurm jobs that never reached the Jarvis runtime against live and accounting state.\\n\\nFITS-HDF5-VOL, released May 6 and written by Muradli, lets HDF5 programs read FITS astronomy files without converting them. It is a read-only HDF5 Virtual Object Layer connector, so tools such as h5py and h5dump open an unmodified FITS file and read its pixels, tables and headers. It is validated against HDF5 1.14.x and 2.1.x with 162 tests, including the astropy test corpus and files from NRAO.\\n\\nCLIO will be shown at the HDF5 birds-of-a-feather session at SC26 on November 19; see [GRC at SC26](/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/grc-at-sc26).\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/)\\n- [IOWarp on GitHub](https://github.com/iowarp)\\n- [IOWarp documentation](https://iowarp.ai/docs/)\\n- [CLIO Agent 0.9.7 release notes](https://github.com/iowarp/clio-agent/releases/tag/v0.9.7)\\n- [CLIO Coder 0.8.0 release notes](https://github.com/iowarp/clio-coder/releases/tag/v0.8.0)\\n- [Jarvis-CD 1.8.0 release](https://github.com/grc-iit/jarvis-cd/releases/tag/v1.8.0)\\n- [FITS-HDF5-VOL on GitHub](https://github.com/grc-iit/FITS-HDF5-VOL)"},{"id":"/2026/11/13/grc-at-sc26","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/grc-at-sc26","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-13-grc-at-sc26.mdx","title":"GRC at SC26: a visitor\'s guide","description":"SC26 runs November 15 to 20 in Chicago. Here is where to find GRC\'s six workshop talks, four posters, one paper, the SPOTTER-AI demo and the HDF5 BoF.","date":"2026-11-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Roundup","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/roundup"},{"inline":true,"label":"SC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/sc"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"HDF5","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hdf-5"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":4.61,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"GRC at SC26: a visitor\'s guide","description":"SC26 runs November 15 to 20 in Chicago. Here is where to find GRC\'s six workshop talks, four posters, one paper, the SPOTTER-AI demo and the HDF5 BoF.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Roundup","SC","AI agents","HDF5","IOWarp"],"image":"/img/sc26/grc-at-sc26.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CLIO in 2026: from agent to marketplace","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/clio-in-2026-agent-to-marketplace"},"nextItem":{"title":"ChronoLog\'s year: from 2.5 to 3.5","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/12/chronolog-2026-from-2-5-to-3-5"}},"content":"SC26, the International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis, runs November 15 to 20 at McCormick Place in Chicago, less than two miles from the Illinois Tech campus. GRC brings six workshop talks, four research posters and one paper, plus a live SPOTTER-AI demo at the DOE booth and an HDF5 birds-of-a-feather session with a CLIO demo. This guide lists every session by day, with times in Chicago time (CST).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Monday, November 16: workshops\\n\\nTwo talks come from work with [Hariharan Devarajan](/center/team/hariharan-devarajan/), a GRC alumnus now at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, at the ProTools workshop in room W176c:\\n\\n- **10:30 to 10:54 a.m.** [Orion](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2026-orion-1c17/), an observability framework for I/O monitoring in hybrid, workflow-centric HPC systems, with [Izzet Yildirim](/center/team/izzet-yildirim/).\\n- **10:54 to 11:18 a.m.** [DataCrumbs](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2026-datacrumbs-11c5/), which captures data across the layers of an HPC storage stack to explain I/O performance, with [Neeraj Rajesh](/center/team/neeraj-rajesh/).\\n\\nFour more are at the PDSW workshop in room W179b:\\n\\n- **10:50 to 11:10 a.m.** [GPUH5](/gnosis/publications/fernandes-2026-gpuh5-9b21/), by [Joshua Fernandes](/center/team/joshua-fernandes/), lets a GPU kernel write HDF5-style data without leaving the kernel. On a Gray-Scott simulation writing 380 snapshots, it hides 96% of the I/O and finishes 1.9 times faster than default HDF5.\\n- **11:30 to 11:50 a.m.** [GDSight](/gnosis/publications/yildirim-2026-gdsight-4e6a/), by Yildirim, traces each NVMe command issued through GPUDirect Storage back to the application operation that caused it.\\n- **12:15 to 12:20 p.m.** A work-in-progress talk on [trigger-gated multimodal LLM agents](/gnosis/publications/xu-2026-trigger-gated-multimodal-1a84/) that monitor simulations in situ, by [Hua Xu](/center/team/hua-xu/).\\n- **4:45 to 4:50 p.m.** A work-in-progress talk on [type-safe, dynamically defined metadata](/gnosis/publications/teves-2026-type-safe-metadata-d6f0/), led by Josh Teves of Sandia National Laboratories with GRC and Oak Ridge coauthors.\\n\\n## Tuesday, November 17: posters\\n\\nAll four research posters are on display from 10:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. in the Skyline Ballroom North Pre-Function area:\\n\\n- [ToolFuse](/gnosis/publications/yildirim-2026-toolfuse-4a7d/) (Yildirim) merges identical tool calls that many LLM agents issue at once. In its test, 16 equivalent requests became one execution and used 11.79 times less CPU time.\\n- [StageOnce](/gnosis/publications/yildirim-2026-stageonce-6b8e/) (Yildirim) stages each input once for a whole fleet of agent jobs on a node, cutting fleet run time by 31% to 51% at 12 concurrent jobs.\\n- [Extending I/O observability to explainability](/gnosis/publications/rajesh-2026-io-observability-explainability-2bb9/) (Rajesh) goes from measuring I/O to explaining it, so optimizations can target the cause.\\n- [GNNs beyond HBM](/gnosis/publications/pawar-2026-gnns-beyond-hbm-3f56/) ([Rajni Pawar](/center/team/rajni-pawar/)) streams compressed, bit-exact features so graph neural networks can train on more data than GPU memory holds.\\n\\n## Wednesday, November 18: a demo and a paper\\n\\n**12:00 to 1:00 p.m., DOE booth 4829, station 2.** [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) and [Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/) demonstrate [SPOTTER-AI](/research/projects/spotter-ai/), which detects tampered data in scientific AI workflows and traces it back to its source. The demo repeats through the hour. SPOTTER-AI is one of GRC\'s two [Genesis Mission projects](/gnosis/articles/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission).\\n\\n**4:30 to 5:00 p.m., room W181abc.** [Trigger, Render, Reason](/gnosis/publications/xu-2026-trigger-render-reason-multimodal-llm-agents-c360/), by Xu, is GRC\'s paper in the technical program. Simulations can run to completion while computing the wrong answer, a waste the paper calls dark waste. Its system, Vigil, adds lightweight science-aware checks to a running simulation; when one fires, the flagged data is rendered as images and a multimodal model judges whether the science is still valid, so the run can be stopped or restarted from a checkpoint. Across four simulation codes, Vigil caught every deliberately configured failure with no false alarms. It builds on the in situ operators Xu presented at [eScience 2026](/gnosis/articles/2026/10/22/autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026).\\n\\nThe paper appears in \\"Papers in Conversation: Scientific AI at Extreme Scale\\", a session that closes with a panel of its authors and is livestreamed, so it can be followed from outside Chicago.\\n\\n## Thursday, November 19: HDF5 birds of a feather\\n\\n**12:15 to 1:15 p.m., room W185d.** [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) joins speakers from The HDF Group, LifeBoat, Ohio State University and NVIDIA for \\"Trustworthy Data at Scale: HDF5 in the Age of AI-Driven Science\\". The session includes a demo of CLIO, IOWarp\'s agent stack, which [grew into a family of releases this year](/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/clio-in-2026-agent-to-marketplace).\\n\\n## Meet the team\\n\\nKougkas also serves on the SC26 State of the Practice committee. [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/) is a coauthor on most of the work above. The [GRC at SC26](/sc26/) page has the full schedule with calendar files, links to each session in the SC26 program, and whom to contact about collaboration, CLIO and the SPOTTER-AI demo.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [GRC at SC26](/sc26/)\\n- [SC26 program](https://sc26.conference-program.com/)\\n- [SC26 conference site](https://sc26.supercomputing.org/)\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/)\\n- [SPOTTER-AI project](/research/projects/spotter-ai/)"},{"id":"/2026/11/12/chronolog-2026-from-2-5-to-3-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/12/chronolog-2026-from-2-5-to-3-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-chronolog-2026-from-2-5-to-3-5.mdx","title":"ChronoLog\'s year: from 2.5 to 3.5","description":"Eleven ChronoLog releases between April and November 2026 added SQL and publish-subscribe access, made writes durable and moved to a new HPC software stack.","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"software-drop","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/software-drop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logging","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/logging"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"NSF","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/nsf"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/chrono-log"}],"readingTime":4.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"ChronoLog\'s year: from 2.5 to 3.5","description":"Eleven ChronoLog releases between April and November 2026 added SQL and publish-subscribe access, made writes durable and moved to a new HPC software stack.","authors":"grc","tags":["software-drop","Release","Logging","Storage systems","NSF","ChronoLog"],"image":"/img/projects/chronolog/design.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"GRC at SC26: a visitor\'s guide","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/13/grc-at-sc26"},"nextItem":{"title":"Autumn papers at eScience and PACT 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/10/22/autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026"}},"content":"Between April 2 and November 12, the [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/) team published eleven releases, from 2.5.0 to 3.5.0. Over those seven months, GRC\'s distributed log store gained SQL, publish-subscribe and agent interfaces. It also learned to keep every event it acknowledges, even through a crash, and moved to a current HPC software stack. This is a summary of the year, release by release.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nChronoLog is a distributed shared log that orders events by physical time and moves them from memory to local storage to an HDF5 archive. ChronoKeepers take in events, ChronoGraphers merge them into story chunks and write the archive, and ChronoPlayers serve replays. The project is funded by an NSF CSSI framework grant (OAC-2104013) led by [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/).\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src=\\"/img/projects/chronolog/design.svg\\" width=\\"700\\" alt=\\"ChronoLog component design with ChronoVisor, ChronoKeeper, ChronoStore, ChronoGrapher and ChronoPlayer, showing write, tail-read and historic-read paths\\" />\\n  <figcaption>ChronoLog\'s original component design. Writes go to ChronoKeepers, tail reads come from keeper memory, and replays of older events come from ChronoPlayer.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Spring: packaging and new read paths (2.5 to 2.8)\\n\\n- **2.5.0 (April 2)** added range, earliest and latest reads to ChronoKVS, the key-value interface, along with a native Grafana data source plugin and a first streaming plugin. It also set up a release pipeline with Docker images and launched a new website and documentation at chronolog.dev.\\n- **2.6.0 (April 24)** split the configuration, shipped source tarballs and RPM and Debian packages, and added end-to-end data-integrity tests.\\n- **2.7.0 (April 30)** separated the client library from the server\'s internal data structures and added a protocol version check when a client connects.\\n- **2.8.0 (May 18)** added extraction chains, which control where sealed chunks go next, and a configurable number of ingestion threads.\\n\\n## 3.0 and 3.1: new ways in\\n\\nChronoLog 3.0.0 (May 29) added ChronoSQL, which queries stories with SQL-style statements, and ChronoPubSub, a publish-subscribe plugin. It moved the ChronoLog MCP server, which gives AI agents access to the log, into the main repository. The release also added a recording service to ChronoPlayer and cleaned up the client API.\\n\\nVersion 3.1.0 (September 7) merged replay streams in ChronoPlayer, let clients read the last N events of a story straight from keeper memory, and added a store plugin for the Lightweight Distributed Metric Service (LDMS), a monitoring service for HPC systems. Version 3.1.1 followed on September 10.\\n\\n## Autumn: never lose an event (3.2 to 3.4)\\n\\nThe fall releases closed the gaps in which an event could be lost.\\n\\n- **3.2.0 (October 2)** keeps each sealed chunk on its keeper until the grapher confirms that the archive holds it. Until then, a chunk the grapher had received but not yet written existed only in the grapher\'s memory. Replays now read events that are not archived yet directly from the keepers, and an incomplete answer is reported as partial instead of looking complete.\\n- **3.3.0 (October 15)** has each grapher record every archive file it publishes in a manifest, and players find files only through those records. On NFS, a directory listing can be stale, so a replay could miss a file written late. Each story also gets its own directory.\\n- **3.4.0 (November 5)** adds opt-in durable writes. A durable write returns success only after the keeper has written the event to a write-ahead log on local disk and synced it, and a restarted keeper replays its log into the archive. With eight writers, a 1 KB durable write took about 480 microseconds at the median on a local NVMe drive, about 15,000 events per second. The unit test suite grew from 480 to 758 tests.\\n\\n## 3.5: a new dependency stack\\n\\nChronoLog had built with Spack 0.21.2 since 2024. Version 3.5.0 (November 12) moves to Spack 1.2.2, with Mercury 2.4.1, Margo 0.24.4, Thallium 0.17.0, libfabric 2.6.0 and HDF5 2.2.0. The newer Margo stops a memory leak and supports deadlines that the next release will use for RPCs that still wait without one. Because the newer Mercury handles requests differently, 3.5.0 cannot talk to 3.4.0 or older, and every component and client has to be upgraded together. Archives written by 3.4.0 remain readable.\\n\\n## The people behind the releases\\n\\nResearch software engineer [Eneko Gonzalez](/center/team/eneko-gonzalez/) led the work from 2.5 through 3.0, including the release pipeline, ChronoKVS, ChronoSQL, ChronoPubSub and the MCP server. Research software engineer [Kun Feng](/center/team/kun-feng/) wrote the Grafana and LDMS plugins and led 3.1 through 3.5, including durable retention, the manifest archive, the write-ahead log and the new stack. Research associate [Inna Brodkin](/center/team/inna-brodkin/) rebuilt the configuration manager and wrote the extraction chains, the player\'s recording service and its combined replay stream. [Shivalika Chaudhary](/center/team/shivalika-chaudhary/), a 2025 summer resident, wrote the first streaming plugin.\\n\\n[Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/) and [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) presented a ChronoLog poster at the NSF CSSI principal investigators\' meeting in Rockville, Maryland, from September 13 to 15. In May, a [GCASR 2026 poster](/gnosis/articles/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters) showed ChronoLog feeding live HPC telemetry to Grafana and serving AI agents through MCP.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [ChronoLog releases on GitHub](https://github.com/grc-iit/chronolog/releases)\\n- [ChronoLog documentation](https://www.chronolog.dev/)\\n- [Keeper write-ahead log](https://www.chronolog.dev/docs/user-guide/architecture/keeper-wal)\\n- [ChronoLog project](/research/projects/chronolog/)\\n- [NSF award OAC-2104013](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2104013)"},{"id":"/2026/10/22/autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/10/22/autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-22-autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026.mdx","title":"Autumn papers at eScience and PACT 2026","description":"Four GRC papers at IEEE eScience 2026 add systems support for science agents and in situ analysis; at PACT 2026, a paper speeds block-sparse transformers.","date":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"ai-data-science","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-data-science"},{"inline":true,"label":"Paper","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/paper"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"I/O performance","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/i-o-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":5.16,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Autumn papers at eScience and PACT 2026","description":"Four GRC papers at IEEE eScience 2026 add systems support for science agents and in situ analysis; at PACT 2026, a paper speeds block-sparse transformers.","authors":"grc","tags":["ai-data-science","Paper","AI agents","I/O performance","IOWarp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"ChronoLog\'s year: from 2.5 to 3.5","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/11/12/chronolog-2026-from-2-5-to-3-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"GRC joins DOE\'s Genesis Mission with Argonne and PNNL","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission"}},"content":"AI agents are starting to do scientific work: finding data, running tools and producing results. That raises systems questions. Does a workflow of agents recover when one of them makes a mistake? How does an agent find the right file? Can storage use the time an agent spends thinking? GRC students answered all three in papers at IEEE eScience 2026, along with a fourth on in situ analysis, and a fifth paper at PACT 2026 addressed transformers on GPUs.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThe 22nd IEEE International Conference on eScience took place in Naples, Italy, from September 28 to October 2, and all four GRC papers appeared in its main track. PACT 2026, the International Conference on Parallel Architectures and Compilation Techniques, ran from October 19 to 22 at the Discovery Partners Institute in Chicago.\\n\\n## Testing how agent workflows recover from errors\\n\\nWorkflows built from several large language model (LLM) agents now run multi-step computational tasks, but little is known about how they cope with bad inputs, failed tools or small numerical errors. In [Agent Error Simulator](/gnosis/publications/bateman-2026-agent-error-simulator-3c7d/), [Keith Bateman](/center/team/keith-bateman/) presents a benchmark framework that injects faults on purpose. It sits as a transparent proxy between the agents and the model and injects format, logic and tool-call faults at chosen points in a planner, worker and aggregator workflow.\\n\\nThe team ran 194 jobs in 10 test groups on four open models from 2 billion to 32 billion parameters. Format and tool-call faults were recovered by every model. Logic faults were not: the 27 and 32 billion parameter models recovered from a wrong value injected into an earlier step, while the 7 and 2 billion parameter models carried it forward. A logic error injected into the aggregator, the agent that combines the others\' results, caused every model to fail. The benchmark suite, injection tools and result data are released for others to reuse. The work is part of the [DTIO](/research/projects/dtio/) project, with [Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/), [Luke Logan](/center/team/luke-logan/), [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) and Franck Cappello of Argonne, [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/) and [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/).\\n\\n## Search that agents do not have to guess\\n\\nLLM agents usually look for files with guesses: glob patterns, grep and file reads, repeated until something matches. This costs tool calls and tokens and ignores the metadata inside scientific file formats. [Acropolis](/gnosis/publications/pawar-2026-acropolis-1f2a/), by [Rajni Pawar](/center/team/rajni-pawar/), adds content indexing to a tiered file system, summarizing each file as it is written, and offers search to agents through an MCP server, with lexical, vector and graph search engines behind one interface.\\n\\nOn a 70-query benchmark over CMIP6 climate data, an agent using Acropolis found files as accurately as one using shell tools, with about one third fewer tool calls per query and about 3.1 times fewer LLM tokens. The work builds on [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/) and [LABIOS](/research/projects/labios/).\\n\\n## Staging data while the agent thinks\\n\\nAgents working on scientific data spend most of their time inside tool calls that read from storage. Before each call, a reasoning model thinks, and storage sits idle while the files the next tool needs start to appear in that reasoning. [AgentStage](/gnosis/publications/yildirim-2026-agentstage-7c4a/), by [Izzet Yildirim](/center/team/izzet-yildirim/), reads the agent\'s streaming reasoning and its file system probes to infer which files come next. It copies them to fast local storage, decompressing on the way, so the tool\'s reads are served from fast storage.\\n\\nAcross four reasoning models, AgentStage made curated scientific tasks 1.74 times faster on average and up to 2.34 times faster in a single run, and sped up tasks from the MLE-bench, KramaBench and DSBench benchmarks by up to 1.36 times. The paper also shows how to estimate in advance whether a task will benefit, since compute-bound tasks or tasks that touch many files gain less.\\n\\n## Derived quantities inside the I/O path\\n\\nSimulations produce more data than can be written as often as scientists would like, so analysis has to happen in situ, while the data is produced. [Hua Xu](/center/team/hua-xu/)\'s paper, [Rich I/O-centric Derived Quantity Operators](/gnosis/publications/xu-2026-rich-io-centric-derived-quantity-8c30/), adds pluggable operators to the ADIOS2 I/O library that compute derived quantities as data is written. Some operators need only the current timestep, such as one that finds vortex cores. Others need earlier timesteps or earlier runs, such as a temporal derivative or a checksum that confirms a rerun matches the original. A metadata layer runs the operators at each step and records every result, with CLIO Core from IOWarp as the I/O manager that keeps history on tiered storage. Each operator declares what it will need, and that drives which data is kept on the fastest tier.\\n\\nThe team tested the framework on three platforms, including the Frontier supercomputer at Oak Ridge, and released the code as part of [Coeus](/research/projects/coeus/). Xu wrote the paper with Cernuda, Kougkas and Sun and with collaborators from Oak Ridge, Argonne, Los Alamos and Sandia national laboratories, the University of Tennessee and Rochester Institute of Technology.\\n\\n## Block-sparse transformers on GPUs\\n\\nAt PACT 2026, Lihan Hu presented \\"Improving Data Reuse across Blocks for Efficient Block-Sparse Transformers on GPUs\\" on October 22, in the session on performance optimization, scheduling and communication. The paper, with [Xiaoyang Lu](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/), Sun and Peng Jiang, reuses data across the blocks of block-sparse transformer models to make them run more efficiently on GPUs.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [eScience 2026 accepted papers](https://www.escience-conference.org/2026/papers)\\n- [PACT 2026 program](https://pact2026.github.io/program/)\\n- [Agent Error Simulator (DOI)](https://doi.org/10.1109/eScience70237.2026.00052)\\n- [Acropolis (DOI)](https://doi.org/10.1109/eScience70237.2026.00045)\\n- [AgentStage (DOI)](https://doi.org/10.1109/eScience70237.2026.00040)\\n- [Derived quantity operators (DOI)](https://doi.org/10.1109/eScience70237.2026.00053)\\n- [PACT paper record](/gnosis/publications/hu-2026-improving-data-reuse-3e1f/)\\n- [Derived quantity operators code (coeus-adapter)](https://github.com/grc-iit/coeus-adapter)\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/)"},{"id":"/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-26-grc-joins-genesis-mission.mdx","title":"GRC joins DOE\'s Genesis Mission with Argonne and PNNL","description":"DOE selected SPOTTER-AI, led by Argonne, and Portus, led by PNNL, among the first Genesis Mission projects. GRC is a co-PI on both and contributes CLIO.","date":"2026-08-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Provenance","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/provenance"},{"inline":true,"label":"Workflows","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/workflows"},{"inline":true,"label":"DOE","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/doe"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":4.37,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"GRC joins DOE\'s Genesis Mission with Argonne and PNNL","description":"DOE selected SPOTTER-AI, led by Argonne, and Portus, led by PNNL, among the first Genesis Mission projects. GRC is a co-PI on both and contributes CLIO.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Provenance","Workflows","DOE","IOWarp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Autumn papers at eScience and PACT 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/10/22/autumn-papers-escience-pact-2026"},"nextItem":{"title":"Three new PhD students join GRC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/17/new-phd-students"}},"content":"import ThemedImage from \'@theme/ThemedImage\';\\n\\nGRC is a partner in two of the first projects of the Department of Energy\'s Genesis Mission. SPOTTER-AI, led by Argonne National Laboratory, protects AI-driven science workflows from tampered data. Portus, led by Pacific Northwest National Laboratory (PNNL), helps coding agents run scientific workflows well on HPC and cloud systems. GRC is a co-principal investigator on both and brings CLIO, the agent layer of [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'3rem\', alignItems: \'center\', margin: \'1.5rem 0\'}}>\\n  <ThemedImage alt=\\"SPOTTER-AI project mark\\" width=\\"96\\" sources={{light: \'/img/projects/spotter-ai/mark.svg\', dark: \'/img/projects/spotter-ai/mark-dark.svg\'}} />\\n  <ThemedImage alt=\\"Portus project mark\\" width=\\"96\\" sources={{light: \'/img/projects/portus/mark.svg\', dark: \'/img/projects/portus/mark-dark.svg\'}} />\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Genesis Mission\\n\\nThrough the Genesis Mission, DOE is building the American Science Cloud, a national platform where researchers share datasets, models and workflows and use AI to speed up discovery. On July 22, DOE posted the list of the first projects selected for award negotiations under the mission. Both projects with GRC are on it, and work on both began on August 1.\\n\\n## SPOTTER-AI: guarding AI workflows from tampered data\\n\\nOpenness is what makes a shared science platform useful, and it is also a risk. A few tampered images or one corrupted data source can quietly skew an AI model and spread through many downstream studies, with no record of where the error entered. Conventional cybersecurity protects the infrastructure, so these attacks on the data itself slip through.\\n\\n[SPOTTER-AI](/research/projects/spotter-ai/), short for Scientific Provenance-Oriented Threat Tracing and Attribution for Genesis Workflows, works in three steps:\\n\\n- **Track.** It records each workflow\'s full history, every dataset, model step and tool call, as provenance.\\n- **Detect.** It asks whether a piece of data should have mattered as much as it did to a result. A transformer model combines provenance with signals such as attention scores to tell legitimate high-impact inputs from tampered ones.\\n- **Trace.** When something looks wrong, an AI agent reasons backward through the workflow\'s history to find the source of the attack and describe how it happened.\\n\\nBecause it inspects only the suspicious parts of a workflow, SPOTTER-AI\'s advantage grows as workflows get larger. The team will first test it on a digital twin from one of the OPAL research projects, which predicts plant traits from images, sensor readings and genetics. Each piece is being built as a reusable American Science Cloud service.\\n\\n[Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) of Argonne leads the project. The partners include Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Hewlett Packard Enterprise and Illinois Tech. At GRC, [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) and [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/) lead the work with [Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/), contributing CLIO Core, CLIO Kit, CLIO Agent and CLIO Coder.\\n\\n## Portus: performance portability for agentic workflows\\n\\nAgentic workflows change their execution paths as they run, generate new pieces on the fly, and must cope with computing resources that contend, fail and change. Without better orchestration, scientists would have to tune every port to a new system by hand or accept large performance losses.\\n\\n[Portus](/research/projects/portus/) starts from the observation that agentic workflows are built from a small vocabulary of recurring patterns. It breaks workflows into producer-consumer building blocks and learns models of how they perform that carry over from one workflow to another. A coding agent can then use those models to find bottlenecks in newly generated workflow code, rank alternatives and map it to the resources available. The models keep learning from live telemetry, because a fixed training set cannot keep up with changing workflows and resources.\\n\\n[Nathan Tallent](/center/team/nathan-tallent/) of PNNL leads the project. GRC\'s part covers performance models for producer-consumer data flow and a storage model for data placement, while CLIO provides context and data services to the agents. Kougkas and Sun lead the GRC work with [Xiaoyang Lu](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/) and [Luke Logan](/center/team/luke-logan/).\\n\\n## Two partnerships, one software stack\\n\\nBoth projects build on long collaborations. With Nicolae, GRC works on I/O for scientific AI, including [PKAS at HPDC 2026](/gnosis/articles/2026/07/14/hpdc-2026-pkas-metis). With Tallent\'s group at PNNL, GRC worked on DaYu and DataFlowDrs, tools that analyze how data flows through workflows. DataFlowDrs was accepted by IEEE TPDS [this summer](/gnosis/articles/2026/07/08/journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs). In both projects, CLIO gives AI agents access to scientific data, tools and context on HPC systems.\\n\\n**Update (November 2026):** Nicolae and Cernuda demonstrate SPOTTER-AI at the DOE booth (4829, station 2) at SC26 in Chicago on Wednesday, November 18, from 12:00 to 1:00 p.m. See [GRC at SC26](/sc26/).\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [SPOTTER-AI project](/research/projects/spotter-ai/)\\n- [Portus project](/research/projects/portus/)\\n- [SPOTTER-AI at Argonne](https://www.anl.gov/genesis-mission/projects/spotter-ai-scientific-provenance-oriented-threat-tracing-and-attribution-for-genesis-workflows)\\n- [Genesis Mission projects at PNNL](https://www.pnnl.gov/projects/genesis-mission/projects#portus)\\n- [DOE selection list (PDF)](https://science.osti.gov/-/media/funding/pdf/Awards-Lists/2026/GM-RFA-Awards-List.pdf)\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/)"},{"id":"/2026/08/17/new-phd-students","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/17/new-phd-students","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-17-new-phd-students.mdx","title":"Three new PhD students join GRC","description":"Ainara Garcia and Anwar Benhnini start with the fall 2026 term on MPI4AI and UniMCC, joining Zia Uddin Chowdhury, who began in January on IOWarp.","date":"2026-08-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"people-milestone","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/people-milestone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hire","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hire"}],"readingTime":3.64,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Three new PhD students join GRC","description":"Ainara Garcia and Anwar Benhnini start with the fall 2026 term on MPI4AI and UniMCC, joining Zia Uddin Chowdhury, who began in January on IOWarp.","authors":"grc","tags":["people-milestone","Hire"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"GRC joins DOE\'s Genesis Mission with Argonne and PNNL","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission"},"nextItem":{"title":"PKAS and Metis at HPDC 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/14/hpdc-2026-pkas-metis"}},"content":"[Ainara Garcia](/center/team/ainara-garcia/) and [Anwar Benhnini](/center/team/anwar-benhnini/) began their PhDs in the Department of Computer Science at Illinois Institute of Technology with the fall 2026 term, which started on August 17, and joined the Gnosis Research Center. They complete a 2026 cohort with [Zia Uddin Chowdhury](/center/team/zia-uddin-chowdhury/), who arrived in January and has already presented his first GRC poster.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', justifyContent: \'flex-start\', flexWrap: \'wrap\'}}>\\n    <img src=\\"/img/members/agarcia.webp\\" width=\\"160\\" alt=\\"Headshot of Ainara Garcia\\" />\\n    <img src=\\"/img/members/abenhnini.webp\\" width=\\"160\\" alt=\\"Headshot of Anwar Benhnini\\" />\\n    <img src=\\"/img/members/chowdhury.webp\\" width=\\"160\\" alt=\\"Headshot of Zia Uddin Chowdhury\\" />\\n  </div>\\n  <figcaption>Ainara Garcia, Anwar Benhnini and Zia Uddin Chowdhury (left to right).</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Ainara Garcia: communication for AI at scale\\n\\nGarcia earned a BS in Computer Engineering from Clemson University. She is advised by [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/) and [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) and works on [MPI4AI](/research/projects/mpi4ai/), an NSF Frameworks project led at Illinois Tech by Nicolae with Tennessee Technological University, the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, and Stony Brook University. MPI4AI extends Open MPI, the open-source implementation of the Message Passing Interface, with native GPU communication, collective operations designed for AI, integration with compute streams and fault tolerance. The project targets three AI patterns: neural architecture search with transfer learning, key-value prefix caching in LLM inference, and large-scale data-parallel training. It also aims to carry its extensions into the MPI-5 and MPI-6 standards.\\n\\n## Anwar Benhnini: memory-centric computing\\n\\nBenhnini is advised by [Xiaoyang Lu](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/) and Sun and works on [UniMCC](/research/projects/unimcc/), an NSF Computer Systems Research project shared with the University of Iowa. UniMCC is building a full-stack system for hardware that pairs near-memory data processors with a disaggregated, shared memory pool. The work crosses layers: architecture, hardware and software interfaces, compiler support, runtimes and performance models. Sun is the principal investigator of the Illinois Tech award and Lu leads the project in the lab. In 2026 Lu was first author on two UniMCC papers about long-sequence LLM inference: an I/O analysis of attention at ASPLOS and a processing-in-memory accelerator for hybrid sparse attention at IPDPS.\\n\\n## Zia Uddin Chowdhury: KV cache survivability\\n\\nChowdhury joined in January 2026, advised by [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) and Sun, and works on [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/). At the 13th Greater Chicago Area Systems Research Workshop (GCASR 2026) at Northwestern University on May 11, he presented the poster \\"Pre-RoPE versus Post-RoPE: Trading Per-Step Compute for KVCache Survivability\\", with Jaime Cernuda, Jie Ye, Kougkas and Sun.\\n\\nLLM inference engines cache attention keys and values so they are not recomputed at every decoding step. The usual approach stores keys after applying rotary position embeddings (RoPE), which bakes each token\'s position into the cache. When a long context overflows and the oldest tokens are dropped, the surviving keys carry stale positions. Storing keys before RoPE and rotating them at attention time avoids that problem but costs compute on every step.\\n\\nThe poster measured both sides of the trade-off with vLLM and Llama 3.1 8B Instruct on NVIDIA GH200 nodes of the Delta system, using LongBench NarrativeQA at 4K to 32K context lengths. Pre-RoPE storage raised time to first token by 10% to 30% and lowered decode throughput by 1.8 to 3.1 times. In return, when half of the cache was truncated, re-rotating pre-RoPE keys took about 30 times less time than the re-prefill baseline, while naive slicing of a post-RoPE cache inflated perplexity by 30 to 65 times.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Ainara Garcia\'s profile](/center/team/ainara-garcia/)\\n- [Anwar Benhnini\'s profile](/center/team/anwar-benhnini/)\\n- [Zia Uddin Chowdhury\'s profile](/center/team/zia-uddin-chowdhury/)\\n- [Pre-RoPE versus Post-RoPE poster at GCASR 2026](/gnosis/articles/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters/) and [poster PDF](https://gcasr.org/2026/pdfs/posters/Chowdhury_Zia%20Uddin_Pre-RoPE%20versus%20Post-RoPE%20-%20Zia%20Uddin%20Chowdhury.pdf)\\n- [MPI4AI NSF award OAC-2514056](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2514056) and [project page](/research/projects/mpi4ai/)\\n- [UniMCC project page](/research/projects/unimcc/)\\n- [IOWarp project page](/research/projects/iowarp/)\\n- [Illinois Tech academic calendar](https://www.iit.edu/registrar/academic-calendar)"},{"id":"/2026/07/14/hpdc-2026-pkas-metis","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/14/hpdc-2026-pkas-metis","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-14-hpdc-2026-pkas-metis.mdx","title":"PKAS and Metis at HPDC 2026","description":"At HPDC 2026 in Cleveland, PKAS schedules LLM inference by predicting KV cache use, and Metis uses LLM agents to explain the I/O behavior of HPC software.","date":"2026-07-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"ai-data-science","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-data-science"},{"inline":true,"label":"Paper","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/paper"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPDC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpdc"},{"inline":true,"label":"LLM inference","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/llm-inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"I/O performance","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/i-o-performance"},{"inline":true,"label":"DeepIO","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/deep-io"}],"readingTime":3.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"PKAS and Metis at HPDC 2026","description":"At HPDC 2026 in Cleveland, PKAS schedules LLM inference by predicting KV cache use, and Metis uses LLM agents to explain the I/O behavior of HPC software.","authors":"grc","tags":["ai-data-science","Paper","HPDC","LLM inference","I/O performance","DeepIO"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Three new PhD students join GRC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/08/17/new-phd-students"},"nextItem":{"title":"Two journal articles on recoverable memory and workflow data flow","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/08/journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs"}},"content":"Two papers with GRC authors appeared at the 35th International Symposium on High-Performance Parallel and Distributed Computing (HPDC 2026), held in Cleveland from July 13 to 16. PKAS, which [Jie Ye](/center/team/jie-ye/) presented on July 14, schedules LLM inference by predicting how much KV cache memory each request will need. Metis, in the REX-IO workshop, uses LLM agents to explain what HPC I/O functions do.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## PKAS: admitting requests with the future in mind\\n\\nLLM inference engines keep a KV cache of attention states so they do not recompute them for every new token. The cache grows with sequence length and batch size and easily outgrows GPU memory. To keep GPUs busy, engines such as vLLM use continuous batching: they mix new requests, which process a prompt (prefill), with ongoing requests that generate tokens (decode).\\n\\nThe schedulers that decide which new requests to admit look only at the memory free for the next step. They do not account for the KV cache those requests will need as they decode. When the cache overflows, the engine preempts requests and later recomputes them, which hurts both throughput and latency.\\n\\nPKAS, short for Predictive KV Cache-Aware Scheduling, runs a low-overhead simulation of future KV cache use and combines it with a lightweight prediction of output length to decide whether a candidate request can be admitted safely. The team built PKAS into vLLM\'s scheduling interface and compared it with vLLM\'s default first-come, first-served scheduler and with a learning-to-rank scheduler. The tests ran on Argonne\'s Polaris system, with Llama 3.1 8B and a mixture-of-experts coding model, on two long-context datasets (NarrativeQA and codeU, with average inputs of 24,000 and 31,500 tokens) and on ShareGPT conversations. PKAS reached up to 7.34 times higher throughput and 8 times lower latency, with the largest gains on long-context workloads where KV cache pressure is highest.\\n\\nThe authors are Ye, Avinash Maurya and [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) of Argonne National Laboratory, Krishna Teja Chitty-Venkata of Red Hat, [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) and [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/). The work is part of [DeepIO](/research/projects/deepio/), GRC\'s research on I/O for scientific AI.\\n\\n## Metis: agents that explain I/O behavior\\n\\nExplaining why an HPC application\'s I/O is slow requires knowing what each I/O function means and what role it plays at runtime, across every layer of the software stack. Experts still do much of this characterization by hand, which does not scale and is hard to reproduce as I/O libraries grow more complex.\\n\\nMetis uses agentic LLMs to combine different sources of evidence about each function, and measures how far the agents agree. Compared with a baseline built on Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools, Metis generalized better to function categories it had not seen (0.90 versus 0.35), finished in 27.7 seconds instead of 84.5, and characterized 41.8 functions per minute instead of 14.28. Under federated scaling, its verifier sustained 330,000 to 1.18 million functions per second. The authors applied it to the behavior of the HDF5 library.\\n\\n[Neeraj Rajesh](/center/team/neeraj-rajesh/) of GRC wrote Metis with Karim Youssef and [Hariharan Devarajan](/center/team/hariharan-devarajan/) of Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory and Sarah Neuwirth of Johannes Gutenberg University Mainz.\\n\\n**Update (July 2026):** On July 29 the lab released [WisIO](/research/projects/wisio/) 0.2.0, its open-source tool that connects I/O bottlenecks in workflow traces to their root causes. Every Python version that 0.1.1 supported had reached end of life, so the release moves to Python 3.10 through 3.13. Small analysis views now run in pandas instead of Dask, which cut one DFTracer analysis from 68 to 19 seconds. Each reported bottleneck now states why it is severe, and a new web app lets users upload a trace and read the findings.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [PKAS (HPDC 2026)](/gnosis/publications/ye-2026-pkas-predictive-kvcache-aware-scheduling-for-81cf/)\\n- [Metis (HPDC 2026)](/gnosis/publications/youssef-2026-metis-7b21/)\\n- [HPDC 2026 program](https://hpdc.sci.utah.edu/2026/program.html)\\n- [WisIO 0.2.0 release notes](https://github.com/grc-iit/wisio/releases/tag/v0.2.0)\\n- [DeepIO project](/research/projects/deepio/) and [WisIO project](/research/projects/wisio/)\\n- [Jie Ye\'s profile](/center/team/jie-ye/)"},{"id":"/2026/07/08/journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/08/journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-08-journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs.mdx","title":"Two journal articles on recoverable memory and workflow data flow","description":"ACM TOS publishes NVREC, a recommendation system that recovers from failure in non-volatile memory, and IEEE TPDS accepts DataFlowDrs for HPC workflows.","date":"2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"storage-breakthrough","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-breakthrough"},{"inline":true,"label":"Journal","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/journal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Persistent memory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/persistent-memory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Workflows","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/workflows"},{"inline":true,"label":"PNNL","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/pnnl"}],"readingTime":3.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Two journal articles on recoverable memory and workflow data flow","description":"ACM TOS publishes NVREC, a recommendation system that recovers from failure in non-volatile memory, and IEEE TPDS accepts DataFlowDrs for HPC workflows.","authors":"grc","tags":["storage-breakthrough","Journal","Persistent memory","Workflows","PNNL"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"PKAS and Metis at HPDC 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/14/hpdc-2026-pkas-metis"},"nextItem":{"title":"Spring 2026 papers: LLM inference meets the memory hierarchy","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/25/spring-papers-llm-memory"}},"content":"Two long-running lines of GRC research reached journals this summer. On July 8, ACM Transactions on Storage published NVREC, a recommendation system that trains in non-volatile memory and can recover after a failure. IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems accepted DataFlowDrs, a suite of tools from Pacific Northwest National Laboratory (PNNL) and GRC that finds and fixes data flow bottlenecks in HPC workflows. It will appear in the December issue.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## NVREC: checkpoints that live in memory\\n\\nRecommendation models hold very large embedding tables. Current training systems assume memory is volatile, so they periodically checkpoint model weights and embeddings to slower solid-state drives in case of failure. That design cannot take advantage of non-volatile memory (NVM), which keeps its contents when power is lost.\\n\\nNVREC trains on a hybrid of DRAM and NVM and checkpoints in memory. It uses three techniques:\\n\\n- It splits the embeddings so that only popular vectors sit in DRAM, which hides the higher latency of NVM.\\n- It keeps two versions of each vector in NVM, one persistent copy for crash recovery and one runtime copy for training updates, and alternates between them.\\n- It uses fine-grained parallelism to hide the cost of checkpointing the model parameters held in DRAM and on the GPU.\\n\\nOn synthetic and real-world datasets, NVREC trained 1.05 to 6.85 times faster than existing systems and checkpointed more than 5 times faster.\\n\\nThe authors are Ping Chen, Shuibing He, Peiyi Hong, Siling Yang, Tong Wu and Gang Chen of Zhejiang University, Xuechen Zhang of Kennesaw State University, and [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/). He, now a professor at Zhejiang University, is a former postdoctoral researcher in Sun\'s group.\\n\\n## DataFlowDrs: diagnosing data flow in HPC workflows\\n\\nScientific workflows on HPC systems move large amounts of data between tasks, and the bottlenecks often appear in storage and I/O networks. Earlier work covered workflow coordination, scheduling and storage systems, but no tool suite could automatically find where data flow was holding a workflow back.\\n\\nDataFlowDrs fills that gap with five parts:\\n\\n- lightweight, high-resolution measurement and visualization of workflow data flow\\n- fast modeling that compresses repeated coordination patterns to shrink the analysis data\\n- interpretable models, generated automatically, that predict how data flow scales\\n- analysis that quantifies and ranks bottlenecks for different mixes of task parallelism and storage\\n- new schedules and resource assignments that resolve the bottlenecks\\n\\nOn a representative set of workflows, DataFlowDrs improved performance over baseline and earlier methods by choosing the right trade-off between task parallelism and storage performance. Student teams reproduced the work in the Student Cluster Competition at SC24, and the article summarizes their findings. The suite is open source and includes DaYu, the HDF5 data flow analysis tool that [Meng Tang](/center/team/meng-tang/) of GRC first-authored at IEEE Cluster 2024.\\n\\nThe authors are [Nathan Tallent](/center/team/nathan-tallent/), Jesun Firoz, Zhen Peng and [Luanzheng Guo](/center/team/luanzheng-guo/) of PNNL, together with Tang, [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) and Sun of GRC.\\n\\n**Update (August 2026):** The partnership with PNNL continues in [Portus](/research/projects/portus/), a DOE Genesis Mission project led by PNNL in which GRC is a co-PI. See [GRC joins DOE\'s Genesis Mission with Argonne and PNNL](/gnosis/articles/2026/08/26/grc-joins-genesis-mission).\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [NVREC (ACM TOS)](/gnosis/publications/chen-2026-nvrec-8e3a/)\\n- [DataFlowDrs (IEEE TPDS)](/gnosis/publications/tallent-2026-dataflowdrs-5e21/)\\n- [DataFlowDrs on GitHub](https://github.com/pnnl/DataFlowDrs)\\n- [DaYu (IEEE Cluster 2024)](/gnosis/publications/tang-2024-dayu-f286/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2026/05/25/spring-papers-llm-memory","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/25/spring-papers-llm-memory","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-25-spring-papers-llm-memory.mdx","title":"Spring 2026 papers: LLM inference meets the memory hierarchy","description":"Papers at ASPLOS, EACL, DATE and IPDPS 2026 attack data movement in long-context LLM inference, from hardware prefetching up to KV cache pruning.","date":"2026-05-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"ai-data-science","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-data-science"},{"inline":true,"label":"Paper","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/paper"},{"inline":true,"label":"LLM inference","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/llm-inference"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"UniMCC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/uni-mcc"}],"readingTime":4.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Spring 2026 papers: LLM inference meets the memory hierarchy","description":"Papers at ASPLOS, EACL, DATE and IPDPS 2026 attack data movement in long-context LLM inference, from hardware prefetching up to KV cache pruning.","authors":"grc","tags":["ai-data-science","Paper","LLM inference","Memory systems","UniMCC"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Two journal articles on recoverable memory and workflow data flow","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/07/08/journal-articles-nvrec-dataflowdrs"},"nextItem":{"title":"Twelve posters at GCASR 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters"}},"content":"Between March and May 2026, GRC researchers and their collaborators presented four papers on one problem: long-context LLM inference is limited by how data moves through the memory hierarchy, not only by compute. The papers appeared at ASPLOS, EACL, DATE and IPDPS, and their answers range from a hardware prefetcher to KV cache pruning. A fifth paper, also at IPDPS, applies the same I/O lens to HPC workflows.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The I/O cost of attention (ASPLOS)\\n\\nSelf-attention, the core of transformer models, needs memory that grows with the square of the sequence length. At ASPLOS 2026 in Pittsburgh (March 22 to 26), [Xiaoyang Lu](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/) and [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/), with Boyu Long, Xiaoming Chen and Yinhe Han of the Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, asked how far careful I/O analysis can take an accelerator for it.\\n\\nThe paper first derives an I/O analysis for tall-and-skinny matrix multiplication, which captures the dominant data movement of long-sequence attention. From that analysis the authors build AttenIO, an accelerator for exact long-sequence self-attention with three optimizations: analytically derived tiling and scheduling that minimize I/O, three-level overlap of communication and computation to hide I/O stalls, and parallel execution patterns for softmax. AttenIO runs 1.6 to 8.8 times faster than the best existing solutions the authors compared against. The authors argue that the method reaches beyond attention, as a principled way to guide I/O optimization in general.\\n\\n## Processing in memory for sparse attention (IPDPS)\\n\\nLLM inference has two stages with different bottlenecks. The prefill stage, which processes the prompt, carries the quadratic cost of attention; the decoding stage, which produces one token at a time, is bound by memory. Sparse attention cuts the cost of prefill, but moving data between processor and memory still limits the GPU.\\n\\nAt IPDPS 2026 in New Orleans (May 25 to 29), Lu and Sun, with Lihan Hu and Peng Jiang of the University of Iowa, GRC\'s partner on [UniMCC](/research/projects/unimcc/), and Hongrui Huang of Columbia University, presented PILOT. It places processing units inside memory to run hybrid sparse attention during prefill and both dense and sparse attention during decoding, and uses I/O analysis to choose tiling and scheduling inside the processing-in-memory hardware. Paired with a GPU, PILOT runs sparse prefill attention up to 19.96 times faster and decoding attention up to 57.11 times faster than the GPU baseline, and speeds up end-to-end long-sequence inference by up to 5.31 times compared with a conventional GPU system.\\n\\n## A prefetcher for memory-intensive workloads (DATE)\\n\\nThere is growing interest in running LLMs locally on CPUs, for privacy and low latency. Attention on a CPU moves large amounts of data between the cache hierarchy and DRAM, which is where hardware prefetchers help, but current prefetchers struggle with diverse and changing access patterns.\\n\\nAt DATE 2026 in Verona (April 20 to 22), [Vadim Biryukov](/center/team/vadim-biryukov/), Lu and Sun, with Zirui Liu of the University of Minnesota and Kaixiong Zhou of North Carolina State University, presented Zion. It combines independent temporal and spatial modules to cover a broad range of access patterns, and uses runtime feedback to keep prefetching efficient under memory pressure. In multi-core evaluations, Zion improved performance by up to 43.2% on SPEC benchmarks and 43.0% on self-attention workloads compared with the best existing prefetchers.\\n\\n## Pruning the KV cache one page at a time (EACL Findings)\\n\\nThe KV cache stores attention keys and values for earlier tokens so they are not recomputed, but it grows with every token. vLLM\'s PagedAttention keeps the cache in fixed-size blocks, and eviction methods that rank tokens by attention score do not fit, because the optimized attention kernels never return those scores.\\n\\nPagedEviction, in the Findings of EACL 2026 (Rabat, March 24 to 29), ranks tokens using the key and value tensors instead. It evicts individual tokens during prefill and, during decoding, removes a whole block only after the newest block fills, so it needs no changes to the CUDA attention kernels. With Llama 3.2 1B and a 1,024-token cache budget, it reached up to 3,020 tokens per second, 37% more than keeping the full cache, and it cut latency by about 10% to 12% across 1B, 3B and 8B models. [Jie Ye](/center/team/jie-ye/) and Krishna Teja Chitty-Venkata of Argonne share first authorship, with Siddhisanket Raskar of PNNL, Sun, [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/), Murali Emani, [Venkatram Vishwanath](/center/team/venkatram-vishwanath/) and [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/).\\n\\n## The same lens on HPC workflows (IPDPS)\\n\\nThe second GRC paper at IPDPS turns I/O analysis to scientific workflows. [Meng Tang](/center/team/meng-tang/), Kougkas and Sun, with [Luanzheng Guo](/center/team/luanzheng-guo/) and [Nathan Tallent](/center/team/nathan-tallent/) of Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, argue that I/O-aware schedulers look at tasks one at a time and miss workflow-level behavior such as file reuse and producer-consumer data exchange. Their workflow-centric method correlates workflow structure with I/O metrics across six HPC workflows, covering 3,500 task instances, 1,387 unique files and 1.5 TB of I/O. The patterns they found point to better task placement, data movement and storage selection.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [I/O Analysis is All You Need (ASPLOS 2026)](/gnosis/publications/lu-2026-io-analysis-b2c3/)\\n- [I/O-Aware PIM Acceleration for Long-Sequence LLM Inference (IPDPS 2026)](/gnosis/publications/lu-2026-io-aware-ri45/)\\n- [Zion (DATE 2026)](/gnosis/publications/biryukov-2026-zion-a1b2/)\\n- [PagedEviction (EACL 2026 Findings)](/gnosis/publications/chitty-venkata-2026-pagedeviction-3f8a/)\\n- [Characterizing Dataflow for I/O-Aware Scheduling in HPC Workflows (IPDPS 2026)](/gnosis/publications/tang-2026-dataflow-workflow-b4e1/)\\n- [UniMCC project](/research/projects/unimcc/)\\n- [Xiaoyang Lu\'s profile](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/)"},{"id":"/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-11-gcasr-2026-posters.mdx","title":"Twelve posters at GCASR 2026","description":"At the 13th Greater Chicago Area Systems Research Workshop, GRC posters covered agent I/O, data discovery, provenance, logging, workflows and memory.","date":"2026-05-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Poster","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/poster"},{"inline":true,"label":"GCASR","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/gcasr"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":4.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Twelve posters at GCASR 2026","description":"At the 13th Greater Chicago Area Systems Research Workshop, GRC posters covered agent I/O, data discovery, provenance, logging, workflows and memory.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Poster","GCASR","AI agents","Storage systems","IOWarp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Spring 2026 papers: LLM inference meets the memory hierarchy","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/25/spring-papers-llm-memory"},"nextItem":{"title":"The case for context engineering in scientific AI","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/02/17/context-engineering-scientific-ai"}},"content":"GRC researchers and collaborators presented twelve posters at the 13th Greater Chicago Area Systems Research Workshop (GCASR 2026) on May 11 at Northwestern University in Evanston. Taken together, the posters give a snapshot of the lab\'s work in spring 2026: how AI agents read and find scientific data, how multi-agent workflows run and recover, how workflows and logs move data, and how inference uses memory.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## How agents read and find scientific data\\n\\n**Correct is not I/O efficient.** [Izzet Yildirim](/center/team/izzet-yildirim/), with [Jie Ye](/center/team/jie-ye/), [Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/), [Xian-He Sun](/center/team/xian-he-sun/) and [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/), traced the file operations of scientific workflows written by LLM coding agents. Of 105 agent-generated workflows across five tasks, 46 passed validation. Even the correct ones issued a median of 3.8 times more POSIX operations and read 6.5 times more data than expert implementations. The conclusion: storage behavior has to be measured directly before agent-written code runs on shared HPC or cloud storage.\\n\\n**Agentic search.** [Shazzadul Islam](/center/team/shazzadul-islam/), with Cernuda, Sun and Kougkas, presented CLIO Search, a retrieval engine that profiles each data collection and then runs lexical, vector, graph and scientific-unit searches in parallel. The same LLM answered ten discovery queries over 341 National Data Platform datasets three ways. All three answered every query correctly, but the CLIO-backed agent used 52% fewer tokens and 91% less wall time than an agent relying on general web tools.\\n\\n**Semantic search for agent discovery.** [Rajni Pawar](/center/team/rajni-pawar/), with [Luke Logan](/center/team/luke-logan/), Sun and Kougkas, embedded search inside the storage layer: file metadata is extracted, summarized by an LLM and indexed when data is written. On 273 astrophysics simulation files and 20 natural-language queries, a vector backend found the right file first 95% of the time, while BM25 keyword search inside the storage system answered in 0.047 milliseconds, 350 times faster than the same algorithm running as an external service.\\n\\n## Running and recovering multi-agent workflows\\n\\n**Speculative dispatching.** Ye, with Islam, Cernuda, Sun and Kougkas, applied speculative execution to the decision of which agent runs next. Agents start ahead of time while the full plan is computed; a reconciliation step keeps correct work, salvages partial matches and discards mispredictions. In preliminary results, the speculative planner produced dispatch plans with 0.50 to 0.78 similarity to the optimal plan at 4 to 10 times lower latency.\\n\\n**AgentRecall.** [Keith Bateman](/center/team/keith-bateman/), with Sun and Kougkas, presented a provenance and rewind system for multi-agent workflows. It records tool calls, messages and prompts, stores them in IOWarp\'s hierarchical storage, and lets a person or an agent roll back to an earlier context when a mistake surfaces. Preliminary results showed rollback improving accuracy by up to 84% over baselines, with tool-call errors easier to fix than logical or formatting errors.\\n\\n**Trigger, Render, Reason.** [Hua Xu](/center/team/hua-xu/) presented work on multimodal LLM agents that monitor scientific simulations with awareness of the science being simulated.\\n\\n## Workflows, logs and streams\\n\\n**Storage mismatches.** [Meng Tang](/center/team/meng-tang/), with [Luanzheng Guo](/center/team/luanzheng-guo/) and [Nathan Tallent](/center/team/nathan-tallent/) of PNNL, Kougkas and Sun, studied genomics and climate-tracking workflows on a production HPC cluster. I/O took 42% to 98% of total execution time, and choosing the fastest storage for each task on its own could make the whole workflow up to 6.8 times slower than a storage-aware choice, because of the data movement it forced between dependent tasks.\\n\\n**ChronoLog.** [Eneko Gonzalez](/center/team/eneko-gonzalez/), [Kun Feng](/center/team/kun-feng/), [Inna Brodkin](/center/team/inna-brodkin/), [Kyle Chard](/center/team/kyle-chard/) of the University of Chicago, Kougkas and Sun presented [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/), the lab\'s distributed log store that orders events by physical time, appends without locks, tiers data automatically and serves range queries. The poster showed two uses: live HPC telemetry in Grafana, and an MCP server that gives LLM agents a durable, replayable memory of past sessions.\\n\\n**StreamGuard.** Hai Duc Nguyen, [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/), Tekin Bicer, Amal Gueroudji, Matthieu Dorier, Chard and Ian Foster, of Argonne National Laboratory and the University of Chicago, presented low-overhead resilience for real-time HPC data streams.\\n\\n**Where files go.** [Neeraj Rajesh](/center/team/neeraj-rajesh/), with Sun and Kougkas, presented \\"Applications do I/O. What if files actually went... somewhere else?\\"\\n\\n## Inference and memory\\n\\n**Pre-RoPE versus post-RoPE.** [Zia Uddin Chowdhury](/center/team/zia-uddin-chowdhury/), with Cernuda, Ye, Kougkas and Sun, measured the trade-off between storing KV cache keys before or after rotary position embeddings. Storing them before costs compute at every decoding step but lets the cache survive truncation without re-running the prompt.\\n\\n**HyPIM.** Jinlin Wu, Ferit Ozdaban, Selvanathan Sadhasivam, [Xiaoyang Lu](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/) and Minxuan Zhou of Illinois Tech, with Yunhui Guo of the University of Texas at Dallas, moved the memory-bound operations of hyperbolic machine learning models into processing-in-memory units, leaving compute-dense work on the GPU. On HypFormer datasets the design ran 3 to 7 times faster than an A100 GPU and cut total energy by an average of 30% for a single inference task.\\n\\n**Update (November 2026):** The twelve posters, with their PDFs, boards and sessions, are gathered on the [GCASR 2026 event page](/events/gcasr-2026/). GCASR posters are not peer reviewed, so GRC now lists them in [Studio](/research/studio/) with its other posters and talks rather than among its publications.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [GCASR 2026 poster program](https://gcasr.org/2026/posters) and [workshop site](https://gcasr.org/2026/)\\n- [GRC at GCASR 2026](/events/gcasr-2026/): every poster with its PDF\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/) and [ChronoLog project](/research/projects/chronolog/)"},{"id":"/2026/02/17/context-engineering-scientific-ai","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/02/17/context-engineering-scientific-ai","source":"@site/blog/2026-02-17-context-engineering-scientific-ai.mdx","title":"The case for context engineering in scientific AI","description":"In February 2026 talks at Illinois Tech and ESIP, Anthony Kougkas argued that context quality, not model size, decides whether AI agents work in science.","date":"2026-02-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Invited talk","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/invited-talk"},{"inline":true,"label":"ESIP","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/esip"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"Scientific data management","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/scientific-data-management"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":3.58,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"The case for context engineering in scientific AI","description":"In February 2026 talks at Illinois Tech and ESIP, Anthony Kougkas argued that context quality, not model size, decides whether AI agents work in science.","authors":"grc","image":"/img/projects/iowarp/IoWarp-HourGlass.webp","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Invited talk","ESIP","AI agents","Scientific data management","IOWarp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Twelve posters at GCASR 2026","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/05/11/gcasr-2026-posters"},"nextItem":{"title":"Jaime Cernuda and Luke Logan join the research faculty","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/01/05/cernuda-logan-research-faculty"}},"content":"On February 17, 2026, [Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/) spoke at the monthly meeting of Earth Science Information Partners (ESIP) on context engineering for environmental data systems. His argument, first made at an Illinois Tech seminar six days earlier, was that AI agents in science fail or succeed on the quality of the context they receive, not on the size of the model.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Infrastructure built for a different user\\n\\nEnvironmental data systems were designed for human-driven batch workflows. AI agents need something else: real-time, semantic access to archives stored as HDF5, NetCDF and Zarr, which are growing to petabyte scale. Kougkas defined context engineering as \\"the discipline of building dynamic systems that deliver the right data, metadata, and tools to AI agents at the right time.\\"\\n\\nThe point builds on earlier GRC work on the gap between scientific data and AI. [DLIO](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2021-dlio-0272/), a benchmark for the I/O behavior of scientific deep learning applications, won the [CCGrid 2021 best paper award](/gnosis/articles/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper/). [Stimulus](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2022-stimulus-4b4c/) (CCGrid 2022) showed that scientific formats such as HDF5 and NetCDF remain incompatible with AI frameworks unless the data is converted by hand. The ESIP talk took the next step and examined the gap between what current storage systems provide and what autonomous agents need.\\n\\n## How context goes wrong\\n\\nThe ESIP talk identified four context failure modes that appear at archive scale: poisoning, rot, clash and overflow. The seminar described the first in detail. In context poisoning, bad data enters an agent workflow and cascades through it until it corrupts the scientific result.\\n\\nBoth talks answered with the same four core strategies for managing context: write, select, compress and isolate. They follow from the definition above: context is delivered by a dynamic system, not written once into a prompt.\\n\\n## From a chatbot to a multi-agent system\\n\\nAt the February 11 computer science doctoral seminar at Illinois Tech, titled \\"From Storage Optimization to Context Orchestration: Why Your AI Infrastructure is Already Obsolete\\", Kougkas gave a live demonstration that contrasted a single chatbot with a multi-agent system. The demonstration used orchestrator patterns, meta-prompting and human-in-the-loop design to show the infrastructure that separates a demo from autonomous science that holds up in production. The talk also asked whether the future belongs to general-purpose agents or to domain-specific scientific systems, and mapped open problems in context quality metrics, provenance tracking and evaluation frameworks. In both talks, the stated aim was to amplify scientific intuition, not replace scientists, by automating tedious data orchestration so researchers can focus on discovery.\\n\\n## Where IOWarp fits\\n\\nBoth talks presented [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/), GRC\'s NSF-funded platform for scientific data, as the systems answer. IOWarp places data across memory and storage tiers and exposes it to agents. Its CLIO Kit component provides Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers for scientific data formats, so an agent can query an HDF5 or NetCDF file through a standard interface instead of a custom conversion script. ESIP published the recording of the session.\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src=\\"/img/projects/iowarp/IoWarp-HourGlass.webp\\" alt=\\"IOWarp architecture as an hourglass: user prompts at the top go through AI agents and scientific MCP servers, IOWarp moves data in the middle, and content engines place it across storage tiers at the bottom\\" />\\n  <figcaption>The IOWarp hourglass: agents and MCP servers on top, data placement across memory and storage tiers below.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n**Update (May 2026):** Kougkas carried the same argument into genomics as a featured keynote speaker at the 7th IFSH High-Throughput Sequencing Symposium, held May 19 and 20, 2026, in Burr Ridge, Illinois, and organized by Illinois Tech\'s Institute for Food Safety and Health. His talk on May 20, \\"IOWarp: Intelligent Data Management to Accelerate Food Safety Genomic Research\\", applied IOWarp to the data handling in genomic analysis.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [ESIP recording: Context Engineering for Environmental Data Systems](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IYkZCcEwXpQ)\\n- [Illinois Tech CS doctoral seminar, February 11, 2026](https://datasys.cs.iit.edu/seminar/kougkas-2026-02-11.html)\\n- [7th IFSH High-Throughput Sequencing Symposium](https://hts.iit.edu/events/2025-symposium.html)\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/) and [IOWarp on GitHub](https://github.com/iowarp)\\n- [Anthony Kougkas\'s profile](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/)"},{"id":"/2026/01/05/cernuda-logan-research-faculty","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/01/05/cernuda-logan-research-faculty","source":"@site/blog/2026-01-05-cernuda-logan-research-faculty.mdx","title":"Jaime Cernuda and Luke Logan join the research faculty","description":"Two GRC-trained systems researchers become assistant research professors at Illinois Tech, and Bogdan Nicolae of Argonne joins the center the same month.","date":"2026-01-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"people-milestone","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/people-milestone"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hire","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hire"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":3.71,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Jaime Cernuda and Luke Logan join the research faculty","description":"Two GRC-trained systems researchers become assistant research professors at Illinois Tech, and Bogdan Nicolae of Argonne joins the center the same month.","authors":"grc","tags":["people-milestone","Hire","IOWarp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The case for context engineering in scientific AI","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/02/17/context-engineering-scientific-ai"},"nextItem":{"title":"The modern HPC+AI researcher: from specialist to orchestrator","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/12/23/age-of-agent-orchestrator"}},"content":"[Jaime Cernuda](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/) and [Luke Logan](/center/team/luke-logan/) became assistant research professors at Illinois Institute of Technology in January 2026. Both trained as PhD students in the Gnosis Research Center and stay with it, carrying their work on storage and data movement into IOWarp, the lab\'s NSF-funded platform for scientific data and AI agents.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <div style={{display: \'flex\', gap: \'1rem\', justifyContent: \'flex-start\'}}>\\n    <img src=\\"/img/members/cernuda.webp\\" width=\\"200\\" alt=\\"Headshot of Jaime Cernuda\\" />\\n    <img src=\\"/img/members/logan.webp\\" width=\\"200\\" alt=\\"Headshot of Luke Logan\\" />\\n  </div>\\n  <figcaption>Jaime Cernuda (left) and Luke Logan.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Luke Logan: storage that programs can shape\\n\\nLogan first joined Illinois Tech as an undergraduate researcher in 2017 and began his PhD in 2020. His degree was conferred in December 2025. His research asks how to make distributed storage and operating systems more programmable, portable and fast, and each of his main systems answers part of that question:\\n\\n- **LabStor** (SC22) is a modular platform for building customized, high-performance I/O stacks in user space.\\n- **MegaMmap** (SC24) is a software distributed shared memory that tiers DRAM with NVMe, SSD and HDD, so applications can work on datasets larger than memory without writing out-of-core code. Logan presented it in Atlanta; the [SC24 article](/gnosis/articles/2024/11/17/logan-megammap-sc24/) covers the design and results.\\n- **pMEMCPY** (REX-IO 2021) is a small, portable I/O library for storing data in persistent memory.\\n\\nHe also co-authored an evaluation of the DAOS object store for simulation and deep learning workloads, first at the CHEOPS 2023 workshop and then in ACM SIGOPS Operating Systems Review in 2024.\\n\\n## Jaime Cernuda: data as streams\\n\\nCernuda joined the lab in August 2020 and defended his dissertation in late 2025. His work treats scientific data as something in motion, moving through tiers of storage and stages of analysis:\\n\\n- **HFlow** (CLUSTER 2021) is a dynamic, elastic, multi-layered data forwarder.\\n- **Hades** (CCGrid 2024) is a context-aware I/O layer that computes selected quantities while simulation data is in transit, so later analysis queries do not have to traverse large files again.\\n- **HStream** (ICPP 2024) is a hierarchical data streaming engine for high-throughput scientific applications.\\n- **Jarvis** (PDSW 2024) is an extensible Python framework that deploys, manages and monitors software on heterogeneous HPC clusters, with pipelines that stay portable across machines.\\n\\n## What they work on now\\n\\nBoth have been on the IOWarp team since the project started in August 2024. IOWarp is funded by an [NSF framework grant](/gnosis/articles/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant/) shared with the University of Utah. It organizes scientific data, metadata, telemetry and computational state so that workflows and AI agents can find and use them. MegaMmap and Jarvis are both part of the IOWarp publication record. Logan and Cernuda are also the two main developers of [Jarvis-CD](https://github.com/grc-iit/jarvis-cd), the open-source tool behind the Jarvis paper, and they now carry that work forward as researchers rather than students.\\n\\n## A third faculty arrival\\n\\n[Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) also joined GRC in January 2026. He is a computer scientist at Argonne National Laboratory and holds a Research Professor appointment at Illinois Tech. His research covers fault tolerance, checkpointing and data management for HPC and AI, and he received a [DOE Early Career Research Program award](https://www.anl.gov/article/six-argonne-researchers-receive-doe-early-career-research-program-awards) in 2020. He leads the Illinois Tech part of [MPI4AI](/research/projects/mpi4ai/), an NSF project that extends Open MPI for large-scale AI training and inference, and has worked with GRC since 2023 as an Argonne partner on the DOE-funded [DTIO](/research/projects/dtio/) project.\\n\\n**Update (May 2026):** Cernuda\'s PhD degree was conferred in May 2026.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Jaime Cernuda\'s profile](/center/team/jaime-cernuda/)\\n- [Luke Logan\'s profile](/center/team/luke-logan/)\\n- [Bogdan Nicolae\'s profile](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/) and [personal site](https://www.bnicolae.net/)\\n- [LabStor (SC22)](/gnosis/publications/logan-2022-labstor-f69d/), [MegaMmap (SC24)](/gnosis/publications/logan-2024-megammap-cbda/) and [pMEMCPY (REX-IO 2021)](/gnosis/publications/logan-2021-pmemcpy-f81c/)\\n- [HFlow (CLUSTER 2021)](/gnosis/publications/cernuda-2021-hflow-2f5b/), [Hades (CCGrid 2024)](/gnosis/publications/cernuda-2024-hades-e18c/), [HStream (ICPP 2024)](/gnosis/publications/cernuda-2024-hstream-3043/) and [Jarvis (PDSW 2024)](/gnosis/publications/cernuda-2024-jarvis-3b52/)\\n- [IOWarp project](/research/projects/iowarp/)\\n- [MPI4AI project](/research/projects/mpi4ai/)"},{"id":"/2025/12/23/age-of-agent-orchestrator","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/12/23/age-of-agent-orchestrator","source":"@site/blog/2025-12-23-age-of-agent-orchestrator.mdx","title":"The modern HPC+AI researcher: from specialist to orchestrator","description":"Anthony Kougkas argues that the scarcest research skill is now orchestrating compute, data, AI agents and expert judgment, not deep knowledge of one task.","date":"2025-12-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"ai-data-science","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-data-science"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"Workflows","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/workflows"}],"readingTime":6.97,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Anthony Kougkas","member_slug":"anthony-kougkas","role":"Co-Founder and Executive Director","title":"Co-Founder and Executive Director, Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://akougkas.io/","socials":{"email":"mailto:akougkas@illinoistech.edu"},"imageURL":"/img/members/kougkas.webp","key":"akougkas","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"The modern HPC+AI researcher: from specialist to orchestrator","description":"Anthony Kougkas argues that the scarcest research skill is now orchestrating compute, data, AI agents and expert judgment, not deep knowledge of one task.","authors":"akougkas","tags":["ai-data-science","AI agents","Workflows"],"image":"/img/members/kougkas.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Jaime Cernuda and Luke Logan join the research faculty","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2026/01/05/cernuda-logan-research-faculty"},"nextItem":{"title":"HPCC 2025 keynote: from parallel computing to concurrent data access","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/08/13/sun-hpcc-keynote"}},"content":"The capability of artificial intelligence to undertake complex tasks is growing at a pace that defies easy extrapolation. For those of us who have spent careers building the infrastructure that makes large-scale computation possible, this moment feels less like a disruption and more like a culmination, the point at which the systems we build must finally become as intelligent as the science they serve. The question facing every PhD student, every research engineer, and every principal investigator in High-Performance Computing is not whether AI will reshape the research process, but whether they will be the ones directing that transformation.\\n\\nThis is the argument I want to make: **the scarcest resource in modern research is no longer deep knowledge of a specific task. It is the ability to orchestrate compute, capital, data, and expert judgment to produce new outcomes.** The age of the specialist executor is giving way to the age of the agent orchestrator, and the researchers who internalize this shift earliest will define the next decade of scientific discovery.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Expertise reimagined: from knowing to designing research loops\\n\\nThe traditional premium on comprehensive domain expertise, knowing every detail of a specific algorithm, every parameter of a simulation framework, every corner case of a storage protocol, is not disappearing. It is being *repositioned*. Deep knowledge remains the foundation, but its power is now amplified most when embedded into AI-driven workflows rather than held exclusively in a single researcher\'s head.\\n\\nConsider the trajectory. A decade ago, the highest-impact researcher in an HPC lab was the person who could write the fastest MPI code, tune the most parameters, and run the most complex simulations end-to-end. That skill set still matters. But the researcher who will have the greatest impact over the next decade is the one who can **design the research loop**, the one who architects systems in which AI agents explore vast parameter spaces, analyze intermediate results, flag ambiguities for human review, and iterate toward solutions with minimal manual intervention.\\n\\nThis is not a hypothetical future. It is the design philosophy behind projects like [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/) and [DaYu](/gnosis/publications/tang-2024-dayu-f286/), where our work has evolved from building tools that researchers operate to building platforms that researchers *direct*. The distinction is subtle but consequential: the former requires the researcher to be present at every step; the latter requires the researcher to be present at the *right* steps, the ones where human judgment, domain intuition, and creative hypothesis generation are genuinely irreplaceable.\\n\\nThe practical implication for PhD students is direct: invest in learning how to decompose research problems into components that can be delegated to intelligent systems, and components that require your irreplaceable expertise. The goal is not to automate yourself out of relevance, but to amplify your relevance by orders of magnitude.\\n\\n## Resource orchestration as a core competency\\n\\nIn a world where you can spin up thousands of AI agents or launch massively parallel simulations on demand, the bottleneck shifts from individual task completion to the **strategic allocation of finite resources**. Your HPC cluster has a fixed number of nodes. Your computational budget has a ceiling. Access to unique experimental datasets is constrained. And your own time, the hours you spend reviewing results, refining hypotheses, and making judgment calls, is the scarcest resource of all.\\n\\nThe modern researcher must become fluent in a new kind of resource management:\\n\\n- **Compute allocation**: When do you burn GPU-hours on broad exploration versus targeted refinement? When is it worth running that 10,000-node simulation, and when is a well-designed surrogate model sufficient?\\n- **Energy-aware scheduling**: As energy costs become a first-class concern in HPC operations, the ability to schedule intensive workloads during off-peak windows or in regions with cheaper power is not just an operations problem, it is a research design problem.\\n- **Human-in-the-loop budgeting**: Perhaps the most underappreciated resource question is *when to invest your own attention*. An effective orchestrator knows which intermediate results to inspect personally and which to delegate to automated validation pipelines.\\n\\nThis is fundamentally an optimization problem, and it is one that HPC researchers are uniquely well-equipped to solve, if they recognize it as part of their job description. Resource orchestration is not administrative overhead. It is a core research competency, and it will increasingly separate high-impact labs from the rest.\\n\\nAt GRC, this thinking is reflected in how we approach projects like [WisIO](/research/projects/wisio/), which automates I/O bottleneck detection across entire workflow pipelines, and [DTIO](/research/projects/dtio/), which expresses I/O as composable, replayable tasks. Both embody the principle that the infrastructure itself should be intelligent enough to optimize resource utilization, freeing the researcher to focus on the science.\\n\\n## Agent literacy: the new foundational skill\\n\\nJust as proficiency with version control, scripting, and statistical software became baseline expectations for researchers over the past two decades, **literacy in designing, managing, and auditing AI agent workflows** will become a foundational skill within the next five years. This goes well beyond prompt engineering. It is closer to product management for AI-driven research.\\n\\nWhat does agent literacy look like in practice?\\n\\n1. **Decomposition**: Breaking a complex research question into sub-tasks that AI can meaningfully assist with, and sub-tasks that require human expertise.\\n2. **Objective specification**: Defining clear, measurable objectives for AI agents, not just \\"analyze this dataset\\" but \\"identify statistically significant deviations from the baseline model at the 95% confidence level, and rank them by potential impact on the hypothesis.\\"\\n3. **Output auditing**: Developing robust habits for evaluating AI-generated results. This means understanding failure modes, recognizing hallucination patterns, and building verification pipelines that catch errors before they propagate.\\n4. **Iterative refinement**: Treating AI workflows the way good engineers treat any system, with continuous testing, A/B experimentation, and iterative improvement. The first version of an AI-assisted research pipeline will not be the best version. The discipline of systematic refinement is what compounds impact over time.\\n\\nThis is why emerging work like the agentic memory direction of [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/), which coordinates memory for multi-agent systems, and [CLIO GenomIO](/research/projects/iowarp/), which develops context-aware AI agents for genomic data orchestration, represents a pedagogical contribution as well as a technical one. They are defining the design patterns that the next generation of researchers will need to internalize.\\n\\n## The orchestrator\'s mandate\\n\\nThe argument I am making is not that deep technical skill is becoming less important. It is that deep technical skill, *by itself*, is no longer sufficient for maximum impact. The researchers who will lead the next era of scientific discovery will be the ones who combine three capabilities:\\n\\n1. **Domain expertise** deep enough to know which questions matter and which results to trust.\\n2. **Systems thinking** broad enough to design intelligent pipelines that use heterogeneous resources, from HPC clusters to AI agents to human collaborators.\\n3. **Orchestration discipline** rigorous enough to manage the complexity that emerges when all of these capabilities are composed into a single research operation.\\n\\nThe capability of AI to undertake complex tasks is growing exponentially. The cost of compute continues to fall. The volume of data continues to rise. In this environment, the premium shifts decisively toward *judgment*, the ability to determine what to compute, when, with what resources, and to what end.\\n\\nAs you progress in your PhD, I encourage you to embrace this shift deliberately. Do not simply learn to use AI tools. Learn to *direct* them. Learn to build systems that make your research more efficient, more rigorous, and more ambitious than any individual, no matter how talented, could achieve alone.\\n\\nThis is the Age of the Agent Orchestrator. The infrastructure is being built. The question is whether you are ready to lead it.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [IOWarp project page](/research/projects/iowarp/)\\n- [DaYu: Optimizing Distributed Scientific Workflows by Decoding Dataflow Semantics and Dynamics (IEEE CLUSTER 2024)](/gnosis/publications/tang-2024-dayu-f286/)\\n- [ChronoLog project page](/research/projects/chronolog/)\\n- [Anthony Kougkas\'s profile](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/)\\n\\n---\\n\\n*Anthony Kougkas is the Co-Founder and Executive Director of the [Gnosis Research Center](/center/about/) at Illinois Institute of Technology, where he leads the center\'s systems-building efforts in intelligent storage, scientific workflows, and agentic data management. His research is supported by the National Science Foundation and the U.S. Department of Energy.*"},{"id":"/2025/08/13/sun-hpcc-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/08/13/sun-hpcc-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2025-08-13-sun-hpcc-keynote.mdx","title":"HPCC 2025 keynote: from parallel computing to concurrent data access","description":"At IEEE HPCC 2025 in Exeter, Xian-He Sun argued that parallel computing and multicore designs worsen the memory wall and proposed dataflow under von Neumann.","date":"2025-08-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPCC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpcc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Computer architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/computer-architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":2.05,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"HPCC 2025 keynote: from parallel computing to concurrent data access","description":"At IEEE HPCC 2025 in Exeter, Xian-He Sun argued that parallel computing and multicore designs worsen the memory wall and proposed dataflow under von Neumann.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","HPCC","Computer architecture","Memory systems","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The modern HPC+AI researcher: from specialist to orchestrator","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/12/23/age-of-agent-orchestrator"},"nextItem":{"title":"PERMAVOST 2025 keynote: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/07/20/sun-permavost-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave a keynote at the 27th IEEE International Conference on High Performance Computing and Communications (HPCC 2025) in Exeter, UK, which ran from August 13 to 15, 2025. In \\"From Parallel Computing to Concurrent Data Access: The Dataflow under von Neumann Machine Approach\\", he argued that decades of work on faster computation have made the data access problem worse.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Speedup is not the problem anymore\\n\\nDeep learning succeeds because it can process vast amounts of data, but computing systems are struggling to keep up, and that has brought back the memory-wall problem. Sun\'s argument at HPCC was pointed. The tools the field relies on, parallel computing, multicore processors and dataflow architectures, were all designed to speed up computation. None was designed for the memory wall, and each puts more pressure on memory systems that are already overloaded. Data access has become the main performance limit of computing, and he called for rethinking computing from a data-centric point of view.\\n\\n## Concurrent data access\\n\\nThe approach he proposed is dataflow under the von Neumann machine. It rests on two GRC models. The C-AMAT model measures the benefit of serving many data accesses at once and shows how locality and concurrency relate. The Layered Performance Matching (LPM) framework uses that measure to tune each layer of the memory hierarchy. Together they shift the target from parallel computation to concurrent data access, which is the move named in the talk\'s title.\\n\\nSun then discussed GRC\'s work in I/O systems, centered on the Hermes multi-tiered I/O buffering system, which uses LPM to decide how data moves between storage tiers.\\n\\n## Shared infrastructure\\n\\nThe talk closed with ideas for computer system designs for AI and big data, and with the role of shared research infrastructure. Sun pointed to StoreHub, a proposed community facility for storage research that GRC is planning with The Ohio State University under an NSF grant, as one way to speed progress.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [HPCC 2025 keynotes, with the abstract](https://hpcn.exeter.ac.uk/hpcc2025/keynotes.php)\\n- [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (IEEE Computer, 2014)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/)\\n- [LPM: A Systematic Methodology for Concurrent Data Access Pattern Optimization (IEEE TPDS, 2019)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/)\\n- [SSDBM 2025 keynote: the data-centric imperative](/gnosis/articles/2025/06/24/sun-ssdbm-keynote/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [StoreHub project page](/research/projects/storehub/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2025/07/20/sun-permavost-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/07/20/sun-permavost-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2025-07-20-sun-permavost-keynote.mdx","title":"PERMAVOST 2025 keynote: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","description":"At PERMAVOST 2025, held with ACM HPDC 2025, Xian-He Sun presented dataflowV as a new paradigm for computing systems and posed open questions in memory modeling.","date":"2025-07-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"PERMAVOST","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/permavost"},{"inline":true,"label":"Computer architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/computer-architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":1.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"PERMAVOST 2025 keynote: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","description":"At PERMAVOST 2025, held with ACM HPDC 2025, Xian-He Sun presented dataflowV as a new paradigm for computing systems and posed open questions in memory modeling.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","PERMAVOST","Computer architecture","Memory systems","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HPCC 2025 keynote: from parallel computing to concurrent data access","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/08/13/sun-hpcc-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSDBM 2025 keynote: the data-centric imperative","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/06/24/sun-ssdbm-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave the keynote at the 5th Workshop on Performance Engineering, Modeling, Analysis, and Visualization Strategy (PERMAVOST 2025), held with ACM HPDC 2025 at the University of Notre Dame on July 20, 2025. His talk, \\"Dataflow under the von Neumann Machine: A New Paradigm for Computing Systems\\", spoke directly to the workshop\'s focus on performance modeling.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## A paradigm for data-centric computing\\n\\nSun started from the shift that big data and AI have caused: computing has become data-centric, so data management is now the first priority of a computing system. Traditional I/O and storage paradigms, he argued, no longer meet that demand, and a new paradigm is needed that joins high-performance computing with the velocity, volume and variety of data that different AI workloads need. He called that paradigm dataflow under the von Neumann machine, or DataflowV.\\n\\n## The models behind DataflowV\\n\\nThe talk presented DataflowV in three steps. The Concurrent-AMAT (C-AMAT) model quantifies the benefit of concurrent data access and shows how data locality relates to concurrency; it gives a way to measure and close the performance gap at each layer of the memory hierarchy. The Layered Performance Matching (LPM) framework then optimizes a memory system layer by layer. On that base Sun defined DataflowV formally and showed results from applying it, including the Hermes multi-tiered I/O buffering system.\\n\\n## Open questions for performance modeling\\n\\nFor a workshop on performance engineering, the last part of the talk mattered most. Sun said C-AMAT and LPM are powerful but leave many basic questions open. Have we used modern hardware advances to their full potential? Should advanced memory systems be built around concurrent-aware locality, a model of locality that also accounts for concurrency? He presented these as research problems for the modeling community rather than settled answers.\\n\\nThe workshop has published Sun\'s slides.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [PERMAVOST 2025 program, with the abstract](https://permavost.github.io/2025.html)\\n- [Keynote slides (PDF)](https://permavost.github.io/files/hpdc25-s.pdf)\\n- [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (IEEE Computer, 2014)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/)\\n- [LPM: A Systematic Methodology for Concurrent Data Access Pattern Optimization (IEEE TPDS, 2019)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2025/06/24/sun-ssdbm-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/06/24/sun-ssdbm-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-24-sun-ssdbm-keynote.mdx","title":"SSDBM 2025 keynote: the data-centric imperative","description":"At SSDBM 2025 in Columbus, Ohio, Xian-He Sun traced GRC\'s path from the Hermes buffering system to StoreHub, a proposed community storage research facility.","date":"2025-06-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSDBM","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ssdbm"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"StoreHub","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/store-hub"}],"readingTime":2.31,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"SSDBM 2025 keynote: the data-centric imperative","description":"At SSDBM 2025 in Columbus, Ohio, Xian-He Sun traced GRC\'s path from the Hermes buffering system to StoreHub, a proposed community storage research facility.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","SSDBM","Storage systems","StoreHub"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"PERMAVOST 2025 keynote: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/07/20/sun-permavost-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"HPC Asia 2025 invited talk: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/02/20/sun-hpcasia-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave the third keynote of the 37th International Conference on Scalable Scientific Data Management (SSDBM 2025) in Columbus, Ohio, on June 24, 2025. In \\"The Data-Centric Imperative: from Hermes to StoreHub\\", he argued that storage systems must be redesigned for data-centric computing and that the community needs shared infrastructure to test those designs.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Why storage has to change\\n\\nBig data, AI and data-intensive science have made computing data-centric, and Sun argued that traditional I/O architectures and storage paradigms no longer meet that demand. Next-generation storage has to connect high-performance computing with the velocity, volume and variety of data that AI workloads bring. The keynote set out the open problems and GRC\'s current approaches to them.\\n\\n## From models to Hermes\\n\\nThe technical core followed the line of Sun\'s 2025 talks. The C-AMAT model quantifies the benefit of concurrent data access and the relation between locality and concurrency. The Layered Performance Matching (LPM) framework uses it to tune each layer of the memory hierarchy. Together they lead to dataflow under the von Neumann machine, an approach that organizes computation around the cost of moving data. The Hermes multi-tiered I/O buffering system puts LPM into practice by deciding how data moves between storage tiers, and Sun presented it as GRC\'s main success in this line of work.\\n\\n## StoreHub\\n\\nThe second half looked ahead to computer system designs for AI and big data that ease the memory wall, and to the infrastructure needed to build them. Sun described StoreHub, a proposed community facility where researchers could co-design, test and validate new storage hardware and software together. Sun leads StoreHub, with Anthony Kougkas as co-PI, under a collaborative NSF Community Infrastructure for Research in Computer and Information Science and Engineering (CIRC) planning grant with The Ohio State University. The planning project was assessing whether the community needs such a facility and what it should provide. The day before the keynote, on June 23, the first StoreHub Community Workshop took place at Ohio State alongside SSDBM 2025, with nineteen student posters.\\n\\n## GRC at SSDBM 2025\\n\\n[Anthony Kougkas](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/), GRC\'s Executive Director, served as General Vice Chair of SSDBM 2025. The conference also published Keith Bateman\'s paper on DTIO, a data stack for AI-driven workflows that GRC developed with Argonne National Laboratory.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [SSDBM 2025 keynotes, with the abstract](https://ssdbm.org/2025/keynotes.html)\\n- [DTIO: Data Stack for AI-driven Workflows (SSDBM 2025)](/gnosis/publications/bateman-2025-dtio-e77d/)\\n- [StoreHub project page](/research/projects/storehub/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [HPC Asia 2025 invited talk, which explains C-AMAT and LPM](/gnosis/articles/2025/02/20/sun-hpcasia-keynote/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2025/02/20/sun-hpcasia-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/02/20/sun-hpcasia-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2025-02-20-sun-hpcasia-keynote.mdx","title":"HPC Asia 2025 invited talk: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","description":"At HPC Asia 2025 in Hsinchu, Xian-He Sun presented C-AMAT, LPM and dataflow under the von Neumann machine as a data-centric answer to the memory wall.","date":"2025-02-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Invited talk","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/invited-talk"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPC Asia","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpc-asia"},{"inline":true,"label":"Computer architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/computer-architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":2.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"HPC Asia 2025 invited talk: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","description":"At HPC Asia 2025 in Hsinchu, Xian-He Sun presented C-AMAT, LPM and dataflow under the von Neumann machine as a data-centric answer to the memory wall.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Invited talk","HPC Asia","Computer architecture","Memory systems","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSDBM 2025 keynote: the data-centric imperative","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/06/24/sun-ssdbm-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"NPC 2024 keynote: Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/12/07/sun-npc-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave the third invited talk at HPC Asia 2025 in Hsinchu, Taiwan, on February 20, 2025. The program lists it as \\"Dataflow under the Von Neumann machine: A Destructive New under Existing Systems\\". Sun argued that the memory wall, not raw compute, now limits AI systems, and presented a data-centric approach to it.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The memory wall returns\\n\\nThe success of deep learning depends on processing vast amounts of data, and computing systems are struggling to supply it. Sun argued that this demand has brought researchers back to the memory-wall problem: processors can compute far faster than memory and storage can deliver data to them. His answer is a concept he calls dataflow under the von Neumann machine, which keeps the conventional architecture but organizes computation around the cost of moving data.\\n\\n## Two models: C-AMAT and LPM\\n\\nThe talk built the concept on two pieces of GRC research.\\n\\n- **C-AMAT** (Concurrent Average Memory Access Time) extends the classic average memory access time model to modern memory systems that serve many accesses at once. It counts how many hits and misses are in flight together and separates the misses that actually stall the processor from those hidden behind other work. This quantifies the benefit of concurrent data access and shows how data locality and concurrency relate.\\n- **LPM** (Layered Performance Matching) applies C-AMAT layer by layer. It tunes each level of the memory hierarchy so that the rate at which it can supply data matches the rate at which the level above requests it, so no layer becomes the one that stalls the rest.\\n\\nWith these two tools in place, Sun formally introduced dataflow under the von Neumann machine.\\n\\n## From software to hardware\\n\\nSun then turned to GRC\'s work on I/O systems, in particular the Hermes multi-tiered I/O buffering system. Hermes uses the LPM framework to decide how data moves between memory, flash and disk tiers, and Sun described it as a clear success. That raised the question at the center of the talk: Hermes is a software solution for I/O systems, so what is the practical hardware solution for memory systems? He closed with open problems and ideas for computer system designs for AI and big data that would ease the memory wall.\\n\\n## The venue\\n\\nHPC Asia 2025 was hosted by Taiwan\'s National Center for High-performance Computing (NCHC) under the theme \\"Chip-based Exploration and Innovation for HPC\\". Sun\'s talk ran from 13:30 to 14:20 and was chaired by Taisuke Boku.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [HPC Asia 2025 program](https://event1.nchc.org.tw/hpcasia2025/program.html)\\n- [HPC Asia 2025 speakers, with the talk abstract](https://event1.nchc.org.tw/hpcasia2025/speakers.html)\\n- [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (IEEE Computer, 2014)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/)\\n- [LPM: A Systematic Methodology for Concurrent Data Access Pattern Optimization (IEEE TPDS, 2019)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/)\\n- [ACA 2020 keynote, where Sun first presented dataflowV](/gnosis/articles/2020/08/13/sun-aca-keynote/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2024/12/07/sun-npc-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/12/07/sun-npc-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2024-12-07-sun-npc-keynote.mdx","title":"NPC 2024 keynote: Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog","description":"At NPC 2024 in Haikou, Xian-He Sun opened the keynotes with a survey of three GRC data systems, Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog, aimed at the data bottleneck of AI.","date":"2024-12-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"NPC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/npc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":2.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"NPC 2024 keynote: Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog","description":"At NPC 2024 in Haikou, Xian-He Sun opened the keynotes with a survey of three GRC data systems, Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog, aimed at the data bottleneck of AI.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","NPC","Storage systems","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HPC Asia 2025 invited talk: dataflow under the von Neumann machine","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2025/02/20/sun-hpcasia-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"MegaMmap at SC24: tiered distributed shared memory beyond DRAM","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/17/logan-megammap-sc24"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave the first keynote of the IFIP International Conference on Network and Parallel Computing (NPC 2024) in Haikou, China, on December 7, 2024. Titled \\"Hermes, Coeus, and ChronoLog: Some big data I/O systems for network, parallel and distributed computing\\", the talk walked through three GRC systems that each attack a different part of the data access bottleneck.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The data bottleneck in AI\\n\\nSun started from deep learning. Its success depends on processing vast amounts of data, and computing systems are struggling to supply data fast enough, which turns I/O into the bottleneck. He argued that data access has become the main performance limit for network, parallel and distributed computing (NPC) when it serves AI, and that AI can in turn help relieve that limit. The talk looked at both directions, NPC for AI and AI for NPC, from the point of view of the I/O system.\\n\\n## Three systems\\n\\n**Hermes** is a multi-tiered I/O buffering system. It optimizes how data moves through a deep memory and storage hierarchy, from memory through local flash and burst buffers to the parallel file system. The abstract describes it as serving both directions, NPC for AI and AI for NPC.\\n\\n**Coeus** enriches the metadata that scientific data systems keep, and uses active storage, storage that can run computation, to calculate derived quantities and answer complex queries over scientific data. The goal is to let scientists ask questions of their data without first moving all of it to compute nodes. Coeus is a collaborative project funded by the DOE Office of Advanced Scientific Computing Research and led by Sandia National Laboratories, with GRC as a partner.\\n\\n**ChronoLog** is a distributed, tiered log store for log data in cloud computing environments. It orders events by time across many nodes, so that logs from large distributed systems can be written and read at scale. ChronoLog is funded by an NSF CSSI framework grant, and its first public beta, version 1.0.0, was released in June 2024.\\n\\nSun closed with the open questions these systems raise and with ideas for computer system designs built for AI and big data. The talk extended the keynote he gave at IEEE/ACM CCGrid 2024 in May, which also covered Hermes, ChronoLog and Coeus.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [NPC 2024 keynote page with the abstract (Internet Archive copy)](https://web.archive.org/web/20260807105402/http://npc-conference.com/#/npc2024/keynote)\\n- [Hermes: A Heterogeneous-Aware Multi-Tiered Distributed I/O Buffering System (HPDC 2018)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2018-hermes-25f4/)\\n- [ChronoLog: A Distributed Shared Tiered Log Store with Time-based Data Ordering (MSST 2020)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2020-chronolog-c458/)\\n- [DOE funds Coeus](/gnosis/articles/2022/09/01/coeus-doe-grant/)\\n- [ChronoLog 1.0.0 released as a public beta](/gnosis/articles/chronolog-1-0-0/)\\n- [CCGrid 2024 keynote: AI and data in computer system research](/gnosis/articles/2024/05/07/sun-ccgrid-keynote/)\\n- Project pages: [Hermes](/research/projects/hermes/), [Coeus](/research/projects/coeus/), [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2024/11/17/logan-megammap-sc24","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/17/logan-megammap-sc24","source":"@site/blog/2024-11-17-logan-megammap-sc24.mdx","title":"MegaMmap at SC24: tiered distributed shared memory beyond DRAM","description":"At SC24, Luke Logan presented MegaMmap, a software distributed shared memory that tiers data from DRAM to NVMe, SSD and HDD for datasets larger than memory.","date":"2024-11-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"storage-breakthrough","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-breakthrough"},{"inline":true,"label":"Paper","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/paper"},{"inline":true,"label":"SC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/sc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"MegaMmap","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/mega-mmap"}],"readingTime":5.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"MegaMmap at SC24: tiered distributed shared memory beyond DRAM","description":"At SC24, Luke Logan presented MegaMmap, a software distributed shared memory that tiers data from DRAM to NVMe, SSD and HDD for datasets larger than memory.","authors":["grc"],"tags":["storage-breakthrough","Paper","SC","Memory systems","MegaMmap"],"image":"./blog-assets/megammap/luke-logan-presenting-megammap-sc24.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"NPC 2024 keynote: Hermes, Coeus and ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/12/07/sun-npc-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"GRC and University of Utah bring IOWarp to the National Data Platform","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/01/utah-ndp-partnership"}},"content":"import ArchDiagram from \\"./blog-assets/megammap/megammap-arch.png\\";\\nimport CodeReduction from \\"./blog-assets/megammap/megammap-code-reduction.png\\";\\nimport Results1 from \\"./blog-assets/megammap/megammap-results1.png\\";\\nimport Results2 from \\"./blog-assets/megammap/megammap-results2.png\\";\\nimport LukePresenting from \\"./blog-assets/megammap/luke-logan-presenting-megammap-sc24.jpg\\";\\n\\nLuke Logan presented MegaMmap at SC24, the International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage, and Analysis, held in Atlanta from November 17 to 22, 2024. The paper, by Logan, Anthony Kougkas and Xian-He Sun, describes a software distributed shared memory that extends memory into storage, so that applications can work on datasets larger than the DRAM of their nodes without writing out-of-core code.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={LukePresenting} width=\\"700\\" alt=\\"Luke Logan at the SC24 lectern presenting the MegaMmap paper\\" />\\n  <figcaption>Luke Logan presenting MegaMmap at SC24 in Atlanta.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Datasets larger than memory\\n\\nScientific simulations and data analysis increasingly work on datasets that do not fit in the DRAM of the nodes they run on. Buying more DRAM is expensive, and the usual workaround, out-of-core programming, makes every application partition its data, schedule its own I/O and synchronize the pieces. When a run outgrows memory anyway, Linux terminates it, and the science stops at the size of main memory.\\n\\nMegaMmap takes the other route: it enlarges the effective memory capacity of a cluster by tiering DRAM with the NVMe, SSD and HDD storage that nodes already have, and manages the data movement itself.\\n\\n## How MegaMmap works\\n\\nMegaMmap presents a shared, byte-addressable address space that spans every process and every storage tier. Each process has a private DRAM cache for low-latency access. Behind it, a shared cache spreads data across all processes and all tiers, and a data organizer decides which tier each page belongs on based on how it is used and how fast each device is.\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={ArchDiagram} width=\\"600\\" alt=\\"MegaMmap architecture: per-process private caches over a shared, tiered cache spanning DRAM, NVMe, SSD and HDD\\" />\\n  <figcaption>MegaMmap architecture. Figure from Logan, Kougkas and Sun, SC24.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nTraditional distributed shared memory systems have to guess what an application will do next. MegaMmap instead lets the application say so. Code accesses data inside transactions that declare a range and an intent, such as sequential read-only access. The declared pattern drives the prefetcher, which stages data before it is needed, and lets MegaMmap pick a lighter coherence protocol for common HPC patterns such as read-only analytics or write-only simulation. Operations on the same page are ordered, so a read always sees the preceding write. Data movement runs asynchronously, overlapping with computation, and datasets can be staged to and from persistent files.\\n\\nMegaMmap is built on [Hermes](/research/projects/hermes), GRC\'s hierarchical buffering platform, which supplies the data movement and metadata infrastructure. For MegaMmap the team extended Hermes with low-latency NVMe access through SPDK, memory-mapped access for CXL memory devices, and data staging for HDF5 and Parquet.\\n\\n## Programming with MegaMmap\\n\\nThe interface is a C++ vector. A program opens a dataset as a shared vector, bounds the DRAM it may use, partitions it across processes and iterates over it inside a transaction. This KMeans fragment, from the paper, reads a Parquet file with a 1 MB DRAM budget:\\n\\n```cpp\\n// Create a shared vector from a Parquet file\\nmm::Vector<Point3D> pts(\\"/points.parquet\\");\\npts.BoundMemory(MEGABYTES(1));  // Limit to 1MB DRAM\\npts.Pgas(rank, nprocs);          // Partition across processes\\n\\n// Begin read-only transaction\\nauto tx = pts.SeqTxBegin(pts.local_off(), pts.local_size(), MM_READ_ONLY);\\n\\nfloat distance = 0;\\nfor (Point3D p : tx) {\\n    distance += pow(NearestCentroid(p, ks), 2);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOnly the data structures that are too large for memory need to change, along with the loops that access them. In the paper\'s applications, the MegaMmap versions were 45% to 2 times smaller than their out-of-core equivalents, because all explicit I/O code goes away.\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={CodeReduction} width=\\"550\\" alt=\\"Lines of code for MegaMmap versus out-of-core implementations of the evaluated applications\\" />\\n  <figcaption>Code size comparison. Figure from Logan, Kougkas and Sun, SC24.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nThe evaluation ran on a 32-node research cluster in which each node has 48 GB of DRAM, a 128 GB NVMe drive, a 256 GB SSD and a 1 TB HDD. The workloads were KMeans and DBSCAN clustering on cosmology data produced by the Gadget-4 simulation code, a random forest classifier, and the Gray-Scott reaction-diffusion simulation.\\n\\n- **Less DRAM.** With 1,536 processes on 32 nodes, KMeans ran with 2.6 times less DRAM, and DBSCAN and random forest with 2 times less, while staying within 10% of their performance with full DRAM.\\n- **Against MPI and Spark.** In scaling runs up to 768 processes, MegaMmap performed close to the MPI implementations and up to 2 times faster than Apache Spark 3.4.1, which also used 3 to 4 times as much DRAM.\\n- **Past the memory limit.** The MPI version of Gray-Scott crashes once the grid grows beyond L = 2688. MegaMmap ran it at L = 3456, a 1.5 TB grid with over twice the data, by spilling to node-local NVMe. Up to L = 2688 it was at least 20% faster than the other tiered I/O versions, because it places data during the first compute phase instead of waiting for it to finish.\\n- **Choosing tiers.** In a 768-process Gray-Scott run that wrote 8 TB, adding NVMe to the hierarchy improved performance by up to 1.8 times, and the study shows how performance tracks the cost of each tier mix.\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={Results1} width=\\"600\\" alt=\\"Scalability results for MegaMmap and the compared systems\\" />\\n  <figcaption>Scalability results. Figure from Logan, Kougkas and Sun, SC24.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={Results2} width=\\"600\\" alt=\\"Cost-performance results for MegaMmap across storage tier configurations\\" />\\n  <figcaption>Cost-performance results. Figure from Logan, Kougkas and Sun, SC24.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Software and support\\n\\nMegaMmap is open source on [GitHub](https://github.com/grc-iit/mega_mmap). The work was supported by the National Science Foundation under grants CSSI-2104013 and Core-2313154 and by the U.S. Department of Energy Office of Science under contract DE-SC0024593. Development used the [Chameleon Cloud](https://www.chameleoncloud.org/) testbed.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [MegaMmap: Blurring the Boundary Between Memory and Storage for Data-Intensive Workloads (SC24)](/gnosis/publications/logan-2024-megammap-cbda/)\\n- [Paper PDF](http://cs.iit.edu/~scs/assets/files/logan2024megammap.pdf)\\n- [MegaMmap on GitHub](https://github.com/grc-iit/mega_mmap)\\n- [Hermes project](/research/projects/hermes)\\n- [Luke Logan\'s profile](/center/team/luke-logan/)\\n- [Anthony Kougkas\'s profile](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2024/11/01/utah-ndp-partnership","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/01/utah-ndp-partnership","source":"@site/blog/2024-11-01-utah-ndp-partnership.mdx","title":"GRC and University of Utah bring IOWarp to the National Data Platform","description":"GRC and Manish Parashar\'s group at the University of Utah are connecting IOWarp\'s data and context layer to the National Data Platform for open science.","date":"2024-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"power-partnership","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/power-partnership"},{"inline":true,"label":"Partnership","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/partnership"},{"inline":true,"label":"AI agents","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"Utah","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/utah"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":0.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"GRC and University of Utah bring IOWarp to the National Data Platform","description":"GRC and Manish Parashar\'s group at the University of Utah are connecting IOWarp\'s data and context layer to the National Data Platform for open science.","authors":"grc","tags":["power-partnership","Partnership","AI agents","Utah","IOWarp"],"image":"/img/projects/iowarp/IoWarp-HourGlass.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MegaMmap at SC24: tiered distributed shared memory beyond DRAM","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/17/logan-megammap-sc24"},"nextItem":{"title":"NSF awards framework grant for IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant"}},"content":"The Gnosis Research Center and Manish Parashar\'s group at the University of Utah are working together to bring IOWarp to the [National Data Platform](https://nationaldataplatform.org/) (NDP). The collaboration is not separately funded; it grows out of shared interest in agentic data management for open science.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The work\\n\\nThe National Data Platform aims to make scientific data and computing resources easier for researchers to discover and use. IOWarp\'s CLIO architecture organizes data, metadata, and computational state as context that AI agents and scientific tools can act on, and the collaboration connects that context layer to NDP.\\n\\n**Update (November 2026):** The first concrete result was an NDP server for IOWarp\'s agent tools, added in August 2025 and now part of [CLIO Kit](https://github.com/iowarp/clio-kit). The two groups are working to deploy CLIO and its ecosystem within the National Data Platform.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [IOWarp project page](/research/projects/iowarp/)"},{"id":"/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant","source":"@site/blog/2024-08-05-iowarp-nsf-grant.mdx","title":"NSF awards framework grant for IOWarp","description":"NSF awards OAC-2411318 and OAC-2411319 fund IOWarp, a data management platform for AI-infused scientific workflows led by GRC with the University of Utah.","date":"2024-08-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Scientific data management","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/scientific-data-management"},{"inline":true,"label":"NSF","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/nsf"},{"inline":true,"label":"IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/io-warp"}],"readingTime":0.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"NSF awards framework grant for IOWarp","description":"NSF awards OAC-2411318 and OAC-2411319 fund IOWarp, a data management platform for AI-infused scientific workflows led by GRC with the University of Utah.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Scientific data management","NSF","IOWarp"],"image":"/img/projects/iowarp/IoWarp-HourGlass.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"GRC and University of Utah bring IOWarp to the National Data Platform","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/11/01/utah-ndp-partnership"},"nextItem":{"title":"DFTracer 1.0.0 released: I/O tracing for AI-driven workflows","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/06/26/dftracer-release"}},"content":"On August 5, 2024, the National Science Foundation awarded the collaborative Frameworks grant \\"IOWarp: Bending the I/O Fabric for Advancing AI-Infused Scientific Workflows\\". GRC leads the project, with the University of Utah as partner institution.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The awards\\n\\nGRC leads under [award OAC-2411318](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2411318), with Xian-He Sun as PI and Anthony Kougkas and Gerd Heber (The HDF Group) as co-PIs. The University of Utah is the partner institution under [award OAC-2411319](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2411319), led by Jacob Hochhalter with Vivek Srikumar. Both awards run from August 2024 through July 2029.\\n\\n## The project\\n\\nThe award abstract describes IOWarp as a modular data management platform for scientific workflows, particularly those that use AI. It targets data access times on hardware such as NVMe SSDs, CXL devices, and CPU-GPU co-designs, and builds on the team\'s prior NSF-funded research on multi-tiered storage, including Hermes.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [IOWarp project page](/research/projects/iowarp/)"},{"id":"/2024/06/26/dftracer-release","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/06/26/dftracer-release","source":"@site/blog/2024-06-26-dftracer-release.mdx","title":"DFTracer 1.0.0 released: I/O tracing for AI-driven workflows","description":"DFTracer v1.0.0, a data flow tracer for deep learning and AI workflows built at LLNL, was released on June 26, 2024; its SC24 paper has three GRC co-authors.","date":"2024-06-26T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"software-drop","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/software-drop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"Observability","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/observability"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deep learning","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/deep-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"LLNL","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/llnl"},{"inline":true,"label":"DFTracer","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/df-tracer"}],"readingTime":0.92,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"DFTracer 1.0.0 released: I/O tracing for AI-driven workflows","description":"DFTracer v1.0.0, a data flow tracer for deep learning and AI workflows built at LLNL, was released on June 26, 2024; its SC24 paper has three GRC co-authors.","authors":"grc","tags":["software-drop","Release","Observability","Deep learning","LLNL","DFTracer"],"image":"/img/members/devarajan.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"NSF awards framework grant for IOWarp","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant"},"nextItem":{"title":"ChronoLog 1.0.0 released as a public beta","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/chronolog-1-0-0"}},"content":"On June 26, 2024, [DFTracer v1.0.0](https://github.com/llnl-asr/dftracer/releases/tag/v1.0.0) was released. DFTracer is a data flow tracer for deep learning and AI-driven workflows, developed at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory by a team led by SCS Lab alumnus [Hariharan Devarajan](/center/team/hariharan-devarajan/), with GRC co-authors [Izzet Yildirim](/center/team/izzet-yildirim/), Anthony Kougkas and Xian-He Sun on its SC24 paper.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What it does\\n\\nDFTracer captures trace data across several layers of the software stack, from the application and its data loaders down to POSIX I/O, so that data loading stalls in training pipelines can be attributed to their source. The [SC24 paper](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2024-dftracer-8fcb/) reports lower runtime overhead and smaller traces than Score-P, Recorder, and Darshan on the workloads it studies, and tracing of dynamically spawned processes in AI workflows.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [DFTracer v1.0.0 release notes](https://github.com/llnl-asr/dftracer/releases/tag/v1.0.0)\\n- [Install pydftracer from PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pydftracer/)\\n- [Read the DFTracer documentation](https://dftracer.readthedocs.io/)\\n- [Read the paper](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2024-dftracer-8fcb/)"},{"id":"chronolog-1-0-0","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/chronolog-1-0-0","source":"@site/blog/2024-06-06-chronolog-1.0.0.mdx","title":"ChronoLog 1.0.0 released as a public beta","description":"ChronoLog 1.0.0, the first public beta of GRC\'s distributed, multi-tiered shared log store, ships ChronoVisor, ChronoKeeper, ChronoGrapher and a C++ client.","date":"2024-06-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"software-drop","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/software-drop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logging","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/logging"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/chrono-log"}],"readingTime":2.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"ChronoLog 1.0.0 released as a public beta","description":"ChronoLog 1.0.0, the first public beta of GRC\'s distributed, multi-tiered shared log store, ships ChronoVisor, ChronoKeeper, ChronoGrapher and a C++ client.","slug":"chronolog-1-0-0","authors":"grc","tags":["software-drop","Release","Logging","ChronoLog"],"image":"/img/projects/chronolog/software.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DFTracer 1.0.0 released: I/O tracing for AI-driven workflows","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/06/26/dftracer-release"},"nextItem":{"title":"CCGrid 2024 keynote: AI and data in computer system research","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/05/07/sun-ccgrid-keynote"}},"content":"GRC released [ChronoLog 1.0.0](https://github.com/grc-iit/ChronoLog/releases/tag/1.0.0) on June 6, 2024. It is the first public beta of ChronoLog, a distributed log store that GRC develops with the University of Chicago under an NSF CSSI framework grant. The release includes the three server components, a C++ client library with Python bindings, and example workloads. The team is asking users for feedback.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Why a distributed log store\\n\\nMany systems now produce activity data continuously: scientific instruments and simulations, sensors on the Internet of Things, financial transactions, HPC job and resource monitors, and the telemetry and traces of distributed software. This data arrives as streams of small, time-stamped events from many sources at once, and it is most useful when it can be read back in order and queried by time range.\\n\\nFile systems and databases are not built for that pattern at scale. A shared log has to accept writes from many nodes, keep a single order across all of them and still serve readers while it grows. Ordering is the hard part. Most distributed logs order events through a central sequencer or other synchronization, which limits how fast they can grow.\\n\\nChronoLog takes a different approach. It orders events by physical time, using the synchronized clocks of the nodes, so that writers do not need to coordinate with each other. Order is guaranteed within the error bounds of the clock synchronization protocol. Log data is distributed horizontally across nodes and vertically across storage tiers, such as persistent memory and flash. Event ingestion is decoupled from persistence, so the servers pull data to lower tiers at their own pace while new events keep arriving.\\n\\n## What is in 1.0.0\\n\\nThe beta ships the ChronoLog server as three components:\\n\\n- **ChronoVisor** orchestrates the deployment. It is the portal for clients, keeps the registry of recording processes and spreads the recording load across them.\\n- **ChronoKeeper** ingests log events from client processes at high speed, groups them into partial chunks of a log and passes them to the next tier.\\n- **ChronoGrapher** merges and sequences the partial chunks from many ChronoKeepers into complete, time-bounded chunks and archives them in persistent storage.\\n\\nTogether they form a working, distributed three-tier log recording system with configurable time-based data management. Events are totally ordered across client nodes within the clock synchronization bounds, and each client\'s identifiers and causal order are preserved. The system is elastic: ChronoKeeper and ChronoGrapher processes can join or leave a recording group while it runs.\\n\\nOn the client side, the release provides a multi-threaded C++ client library for highly concurrent event ingestion, Python bindings for that library, a command-line admin tool, and example client applications that simulate different logging workloads.\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nChronoLog 1.0.0 is marked as a pre-release on GitHub. It is meant for users who want to try the system on their own workloads and report problems, not yet for production use. Documentation and deployment instructions are on the project wiki and at chronolog.dev.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [ChronoLog 1.0.0 release notes](https://github.com/grc-iit/ChronoLog/releases/tag/1.0.0)\\n- [ChronoLog documentation](https://chronolog.dev)\\n- [ChronoLog: A Distributed Shared Tiered Log Store with Time-based Data Ordering (MSST 2020)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2020-chronolog-c458/)\\n- [NSF awards CSSI framework grant for ChronoLog](/gnosis/articles/2021/05/11/chronolog-nsf-grant/)\\n- [ChronoLog project page](/research/projects/chronolog/)"},{"id":"/2024/05/07/sun-ccgrid-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/05/07/sun-ccgrid-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2024-05-07-sun-ccgrid-keynote.mdx","title":"CCGrid 2024 keynote: AI and data in computer system research","description":"At CCGrid 2024 in Philadelphia, Xian-He Sun\'s keynote argued that data access now limits system scalability, drawing on Hermes, ChronoLog and Coeus.","date":"2024-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"CCGrid","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/cc-grid"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":3.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"CCGrid 2024 keynote: AI and data in computer system research","description":"At CCGrid 2024 in Philadelphia, Xian-He Sun\'s keynote argued that data access now limits system scalability, drawing on Hermes, ChronoLog and Coeus.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","CCGrid","Storage systems","Hermes"],"image":"./blog-assets/sun/image1.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"ChronoLog 1.0.0 released as a public beta","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/chronolog-1-0-0"},"nextItem":{"title":"CARE cache management listed in BenchCouncil Chip100 for 2022-2023","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/12/07/care-benchcouncil-chip100"}},"content":"import Image1 from \\"./blog-assets/sun/image1.png\\";\\nimport Image2 from \\"./blog-assets/sun/image2.png\\";\\n\\nXian-He Sun gave a keynote at the 24th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Cluster, Cloud and Internet Computing (CCGrid 2024) in Philadelphia on May 7, 2024. In \\"AI & Data: Challenges and Opportunities in Computer System Research\\", he argued that data access, not computation, now limits how far computer systems scale, and showed three GRC systems built to address it.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={Image1} width=\\"300\\" alt=\\"Xian-He Sun at the lectern delivering the CCGrid 2024 keynote\\" />\\n  <figcaption>Xian-He Sun delivering the keynote at CCGrid 2024.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## The storage wall\\n\\nBig data, AI and other data-driven applications produce massive amounts of data and new demands for data discovery. They have made computing data-centric. Disk-based storage, however, has improved far more slowly than processors and memory, which leaves a wide I/O performance gap. Data discovery also needs new forms of data, which puts further strain on memory and storage systems.\\n\\nSun described this as a storage wall. It calls for performance analysis that puts data throughput ahead of floating-point operations, and for redesigns across operating systems, compilers and hardware that exploit new storage technologies through data tiering, compression, indexing and prefetching. He also noted that data systems are harder to design than computing systems.\\n\\n<figure>\\n  <img src={Image2} width=\\"400\\" alt=\\"Keynote slide on the memory-wall problem: processor performance grew far faster than memory and storage\\" />\\n  <figcaption>A keynote slide on the memory-wall problem.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Models for the memory hierarchy\\n\\nThe talk drew on two GRC models of memory performance:\\n\\n- The [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (C-AMAT)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/) model, published in IEEE Computer in 2014, describes memory performance in systems that serve many accesses at once. It separates the miss penalties that concurrency hides from those that still stall the processor.\\n- [Layered Performance Matching (LPM)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/), published in IEEE TPDS in 2019, uses C-AMAT to tune each layer of a memory or storage hierarchy so that it keeps pace with the layer above. The aim is cache-like performance with the capacity of the lower layers.\\n\\n## Three systems\\n\\nSun organized the systems part of the talk around three problems:\\n\\n- **HPC I/O.** [Hermes](/research/projects/hermes/) is an intelligent, multi-tiered, dynamic and distributed I/O buffering system that applies C-AMAT and LPM to place data across memory, NVMe, burst buffers and the parallel file system. It is supported by NSF and has been released as open source with the HDF5 library.\\n- **Active data in the cloud.** [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/), an NSF CSSI project, manages activity and log data that is written and read continuously across a distributed environment.\\n- **Metadata.** [Coeus](/research/projects/coeus/), a DOE ASCR project, enriches the metadata that manages scientific data so that it can support scientific insight and complex queries.\\n\\nHe closed with the wider question of how information should be represented for learning and data discovery, and with the research opportunities that the AI and big data era opens for computer scientists and engineers.\\n\\n## The session\\n\\nThe keynote opened the morning of Tuesday, May 7, from 9 to 10 a.m. in the Symphony Ballroom, chaired by Jie Wu. CCGrid 2024 ran from May 6 to 9.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [CCGrid 2024 keynotes, with the abstract](https://2024.ccgrid-conference.org/keynotes/)\\n- [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (IEEE Computer, 2014)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/)\\n- [LPM: A Systematic Methodology for Concurrent Data Access Pattern Optimization (IEEE TPDS, 2019)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/)\\n- Project pages: [Hermes](/research/projects/hermes/), [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/), [Coeus](/research/projects/coeus/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2023/12/07/care-benchcouncil-chip100","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/12/07/care-benchcouncil-chip100","source":"@site/blog/2023-12-07-care-benchcouncil-chip100.mdx","title":"CARE cache management listed in BenchCouncil Chip100 for 2022-2023","description":"BenchCouncil\'s Chip100 list of top chip achievements for 2022-2023 includes CARE, a concurrency-aware cache management framework by Xiaoyang Lu and Xian-He Sun.","date":"2023-12-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"},{"inline":true,"label":"Caching","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/caching"},{"inline":true,"label":"Computer architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/computer-architecture"}],"readingTime":0.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"CARE cache management listed in BenchCouncil Chip100 for 2022-2023","description":"BenchCouncil\'s Chip100 list of top chip achievements for 2022-2023 includes CARE, a concurrency-aware cache management framework by Xiaoyang Lu and Xian-He Sun.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award","Caching","Computer architecture"],"image":"/img/members/lu.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CCGrid 2024 keynote: AI and data in computer system research","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2024/05/07/sun-ccgrid-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"Introducing the Gnosis Research Center","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/10/25/introducing-grc"}},"content":"The International Open Benchmark Council (BenchCouncil) lists \\"CARE Cache Management\\" by Xiaoyang Lu and Xian-He Sun of Illinois Institute of Technology among its [Chip100 Top Chip Achievements for 2022-2023](https://www.benchcouncil.org/evaluation/chips/annual.html). The list was online by December 7, 2023, the date of its earliest [archived copy](https://web.archive.org/web/20231207172902/https://www.benchcouncil.org/evaluation/chips/annual.html). BenchCouncil describes the evaluation report as open for comments and subject to revision.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The work\\n\\nCARE is a concurrency-aware cache management framework published at HPCA 2023 by Xiaoyang Lu, Rujia Wang, and Xian-He Sun: [CARE: A Concurrency-Aware Enhanced Lightweight Cache Management Framework](/gnosis/publications/lu-2023-care-bc99/). It starts from the observation that data access concurrency, in addition to locality, matters for cache performance, since a lower miss rate does not always mean better overall performance. CARE introduces the pure miss contribution (PMC) metric, which measures the cost of each in-flight miss while accounting for concurrency, and uses it to manage the cache.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Xiaoyang Lu\'s profile](/center/team/xiaoyang-lu/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2023/10/25/introducing-grc","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/10/25/introducing-grc","source":"@site/blog/2023-10-25-introducing-grc.mdx","title":"Introducing the Gnosis Research Center","description":"On April 23, 2023, the Scalable Computing Software Lab at Illinois Tech became the Gnosis Research Center. This is its history, name, mission and team.","date":"2023-10-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Founding","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/founding"}],"readingTime":3.25,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null},{"name":"Anthony Kougkas","member_slug":"anthony-kougkas","role":"Co-Founder and Executive Director","title":"Co-Founder and Executive Director, Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://akougkas.io/","socials":{"email":"mailto:akougkas@illinoistech.edu"},"imageURL":"/img/members/kougkas.webp","key":"akougkas","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Introducing the Gnosis Research Center","description":"On April 23, 2023, the Scalable Computing Software Lab at Illinois Tech became the Gnosis Research Center. This is its history, name, mission and team.","authors":["grc","akougkas"],"tags":["knowledge-sharing","Founding"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"CARE cache management listed in BenchCouncil Chip100 for 2022-2023","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/12/07/care-benchcouncil-chip100"},"nextItem":{"title":"DOE funds DTIO for computational storage and asynchronous I/O","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/dtio-doe-grant"}},"content":"After two decades of research in scalable computing and data-centric systems, the Scalable Computing Software (SCS) Laboratory became the Gnosis Research Center (GRC) at Illinois Institute of Technology on April 23, 2023. This post introduces the center.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## From SCS Lab to GRC\\n\\nXian-He Sun founded the Scalable Computing Software (SCS) Laboratory at Louisiana State University and brought it to Illinois Tech when he joined the Department of Computer Science in August 1999. His theoretical work includes the Sun-Ni Law for scalable parallel computing (1990) and, at Illinois Tech, the Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (C-AMAT) framework for memory system evaluation.\\n\\nOver the next two decades, the lab\'s scope expanded from theoretical models into systems research. Hermes brought multi-tiered I/O buffering. LABIOS bridged the gap between HPC and Big Data storage paradigms, earning the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019. ChronoLog introduced a distributed log store for scientific activity data. Each project reflected a consistent thesis: data movement, not computation, is the bottleneck of modern science.\\n\\nBy 2023, the operation had grown well beyond a single-PI lab. Research scientists, software engineers, and doctoral researchers were working across a portfolio of federally funded projects spanning NSF, DOE, LLNL, Argonne, and Pacific Northwest National Laboratory. The scope demanded a new structure.\\n\\n## Why \\"Gnosis\\"\\n\\n\u03b3\u03bd\u1ff6\u03c3\u03b9\u03c2 (gn\u014dsis) is Greek for knowledge gained through investigation. The name captures what this center does: we produce knowledge by constructing and deploying the storage, I/O, and memory systems that scientific computing depends on.\\n\\n## What GRC does\\n\\nGRC develops advanced solutions for high-performance computational capabilities at the intersection of scalable computing and data-driven discovery. Three objectives define the center\'s mission:\\n\\n**Advance scalable computing technologies.** GRC builds middleware, runtime systems, and storage architectures for modern scientific workloads. Projects like [IOWarp](/research/projects/iowarp/) and [Hermes](/research/projects/hermes/) directly address the I/O performance gap that limits computational science.\\n\\n**Develop tools for data-driven domains.** Time-series analytics, workflow optimization, deep learning I/O pipelines. Projects like [ChronoLog](/research/projects/chronolog/) and [DeepIO](/research/projects/deepio/) serve the growing convergence of HPC, AI, and Big Data.\\n\\n**Bridge researchers and infrastructure.** GRC connects data scientists who need performance with systems architects who build it. The center\'s open-source releases, benchmarks, and national lab partnerships make that bridge tangible.\\n\\n## The team\\n\\nXian-He Sun serves as Founding Director, bringing the theoretical foundations and institutional relationships built since 1999 at Illinois Tech. Anthony Kougkas serves as Co-Founder and Executive Director and heads the systems-building effort that turned theory into funded software projects.\\n\\nThe research team includes PhD candidates, research scientists, and software engineers working across storage systems, memory architectures, deep learning I/O, and distributed computing. GRC also maintains active collaborations with Argonne National Laboratory, Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, and industry partners.\\n\\n## Open by default\\n\\nEvery major GRC system ships as open-source software. Hermes, ChronoLog, IOWarp, and LABIOS are all publicly available, and GRC researchers co-developed the DLIO benchmark with Argonne. This is deliberate: the center\'s mission requires that the tools we build reach the researchers who need them.\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nGRC\'s current portfolio spans intelligent I/O buffering, distributed time-series storage, deep learning data pipelines, memory system optimization, and large-scale workflow orchestration. As scientific computing enters an era where AI, HPC, and Big Data workloads converge on shared infrastructure, the need for principled data-centric systems research will only grow.\\n\\nWe built GRC to meet that need. Welcome.\\n\\n**Update (November 2026):** IOWarp, named above as a GRC project and open-source release, came later. It began in August 2024 with [two NSF awards](/gnosis/articles/2024/08/05/iowarp-nsf-grant/). The mentions are kept so the post matches the project pages it links.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [About GRC](/center/about/)\\n- [Our team](/center/team/)\\n- [Active projects](/research/projects/)"},{"id":"/2023/08/07/dtio-doe-grant","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/dtio-doe-grant","source":"@site/blog/2023-08-07-dtio-doe-grant.mdx","title":"DOE funds DTIO for computational storage and asynchronous I/O","description":"DOE\'s FAIR program funded DTIO, which expresses data movement as composable data tasks to bring computational storage and asynchronous I/O to HPC and AI.","date":"2023-08-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"DOE","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/doe"},{"inline":true,"label":"DTIO","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/dtio"}],"readingTime":0.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"DOE funds DTIO for computational storage and asynchronous I/O","description":"DOE\'s FAIR program funded DTIO, which expresses data movement as composable data tasks to bring computational storage and asynchronous I/O to HPC and AI.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Storage systems","DOE","DTIO"],"image":"/img/projects/dtio/dtio-arch.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Introducing the Gnosis Research Center","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/10/25/introducing-grc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun named to the ICPP Wall of Fame","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/sun-icpp-wall-of-fame"}},"content":"The Department of Energy\'s FAIR program has announced an [award to Illinois Tech](https://science.osti.gov/-/media/funding/pdf/Awards-Lists/2931-FAIR-Award-List.pdf) for \\"DTIO: Enabling Computational Storage using Data-Tasks and Asynchronous I/O\\", with Xian-He Sun as PI. The same list names Argonne National Laboratory as a partner on the project, with Franck Cappello and [Bogdan Nicolae](/center/team/bogdan-nicolae/).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The project\\n\\nDTIO is a task-based I/O runtime. Its DataTask abstraction expresses data movement, ordering, and dependencies as composable tasks, and the runtime schedules format translation, staging, caching, and replay across HPC, Big Data, and ML data stacks.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [DTIO project page](/research/projects/dtio/)"},{"id":"/2023/08/07/sun-icpp-wall-of-fame","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/sun-icpp-wall-of-fame","source":"@site/blog/2023-08-07-sun-icpp-wall-of-fame.mdx","title":"Xian-He Sun named to the ICPP Wall of Fame","description":"Xian-He Sun was named to the ICPP Wall of Fame, which honors top contributors over the first 50 years of the International Conference on Parallel Processing.","date":"2023-08-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"},{"inline":true,"label":"ICPP","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/icpp"}],"readingTime":0.69,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Xian-He Sun named to the ICPP Wall of Fame","description":"Xian-He Sun was named to the ICPP Wall of Fame, which honors top contributors over the first 50 years of the International Conference on Parallel Processing.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award","ICPP"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DOE funds DTIO for computational storage and asynchronous I/O","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/dtio-doe-grant"},"nextItem":{"title":"LABIOS label-based I/O receives a US patent","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/04/18/labios-patent"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun has been named to the ICPP Wall of Fame as one of the top 50 contributors in the first 50 years of the International Conference on Parallel Processing.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Sun and ICPP\\n\\nICPP is one of the longest-running conferences in parallel computing. Its 50th meeting took place in 2021, with Sun as a [general chair](http://www.cs.iit.edu/~scs/sun/biography.html). His ICPP record includes the Best Paper Award at ICPP 2001 in Valencia, Spain, with Kasidit Chanchio.\\n\\nHis research spans the memory-bounded speedup model known as Sun-Ni\'s Law (1990), the C-AMAT model of concurrent memory access published in IEEE Computer in 2014, and parallel I/O systems for hierarchical storage, the line of work the Gnosis Research Center continues.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2023/04/18/labios-patent","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/04/18/labios-patent","source":"@site/blog/2023-04-18-labios-patent.mdx","title":"LABIOS label-based I/O receives a US patent","description":"US Patent 11,630,834 B2, granted to Illinois Tech on April 18, 2023, covers the label-based I/O method behind LABIOS, invented by Kougkas, Devarajan and Sun.","date":"2023-04-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Patent","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/patent"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"LABIOS","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/labios"}],"readingTime":0.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"LABIOS label-based I/O receives a US patent","description":"US Patent 11,630,834 B2, granted to Illinois Tech on April 18, 2023, covers the label-based I/O method behind LABIOS, invented by Kougkas, Devarajan and Sun.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Patent","Storage systems","LABIOS"],"image":"/img/projects/labios/as_Storage.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun named to the ICPP Wall of Fame","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/08/07/sun-icpp-wall-of-fame"},"nextItem":{"title":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta released","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/03/06/hermes-0.9.8-beta"}},"content":"On April 18, 2023, the US Patent and Trademark Office granted [U.S. Patent 11,630,834 B2](https://patents.google.com/patent/US11630834B2/en), \\"Label-Based Data Representation I/O Process and System\\", to Illinois Institute of Technology. The inventors are Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan, and Xian-He Sun, and the patent covers the label-based I/O method behind LABIOS, which received the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What the patent covers\\n\\nThe patent describes a system and method for executing I/O tasks in a distributed computing system. The operation instructions of each I/O request are separated from its data and represented as a data label, which carries a unique identifier, the source or destination of the data, and the operation. Labels are pushed into a distributed label queue and dispatched to worker nodes according to a scheduling policy, and the workers execute the I/O tasks independently of the client applications.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Patent page](/gnosis/patents/label-based-io-patent/)\\n- [Google Patents entry](https://patents.google.com/patent/US11630834B2/en)\\n- [LABIOS project page](/research/projects/labios/)"},{"id":"/2023/03/06/hermes-0.9.8-beta","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/03/06/hermes-0.9.8-beta","source":"@site/blog/2023-03-06-hermes-0.9.8-beta.mdx","title":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta released","description":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta, from the SCS Lab and The HDF Group, added tags and traits for grouping blobs and no longer requires clients to follow MPI design patterns.","date":"2023-03-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"software-drop","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/software-drop"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"Data buffering","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/data-buffering"},{"inline":true,"label":"The HDF Group","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/the-hdf-group"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":0.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta released","description":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta, from the SCS Lab and The HDF Group, added tags and traits for grouping blobs and no longer requires clients to follow MPI design patterns.","authors":"grc","tags":["software-drop","Release","Data buffering","The HDF Group","Hermes"],"image":"/img/projects/hermes/architecture.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"LABIOS label-based I/O receives a US patent","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/04/18/labios-patent"},"nextItem":{"title":"DOE funds Coeus for accelerating scientific insights","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/09/01/coeus-doe-grant"}},"content":"On March 6, 2023, the Hermes team at the Scalable Computing Software (SCS) Lab and The HDF Group published [Hermes v0.9.8-beta](https://github.com/HDFGroup/hermes/releases/tag/v0.9.8-beta), a beta of the heterogeneous-aware, multi-tiered I/O buffering system first described at HPDC 2018.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What is new\\n\\nThe release notes list three changes:\\n\\n- **Tags.** Users can associate related blobs under a tag and locate them through it. Hermes can also use tags to inform data placement for logically grouped data.\\n- **Traits.** Traits attached to a tag apply a set of operations to every blob in the group, for example compressing and encrypting it.\\n- **Portability.** Client programs no longer have to follow MPI design patterns, and installation issues on recent operating system versions are fixed.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Hermes releases on GitHub](https://github.com/HDFGroup/hermes/releases)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)"},{"id":"/2022/09/01/coeus-doe-grant","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/09/01/coeus-doe-grant","source":"@site/blog/2022-09-01-coeus-doe-grant.mdx","title":"DOE funds Coeus for accelerating scientific insights","description":"DOE ASCR selected Coeus in its 2022 data management awards, a project of Illinois Tech, Sandia and ORNL that computes derived quantities in the I/O path.","date":"2022-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Metadata","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/metadata"},{"inline":true,"label":"DOE","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/doe"},{"inline":true,"label":"Coeus","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/coeus"}],"readingTime":0.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"DOE funds Coeus for accelerating scientific insights","description":"DOE ASCR selected Coeus in its 2022 data management awards, a project of Illinois Tech, Sandia and ORNL that computes derived quantities in the I/O path.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Metadata","DOE","Coeus"],"image":"/img/projects/coeus/coeus_arch.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hermes v0.9.8-beta released","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2023/03/06/hermes-0.9.8-beta"},"nextItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun receives the MSU CSE Distinguished Alumni Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/05/07/sun-msu-distinguished-alumni"}},"content":"The DOE Office of Advanced Scientific Computing Research has selected Coeus in its [2022 data management awards](https://science.osti.gov/-/media/ascr/pdf/awards/Data-Management-Awards-List-2022.pdf). Coeus is a collaborative project of Illinois Tech (Xian-He Sun), Sandia National Laboratories (Jay Lofstead), and Oak Ridge National Laboratory (Scott Klasky).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The project\\n\\nCoeus embeds computation in the I/O path. Through an ADIOS2 plugin, it computes derived quantities while a simulation produces its data, places the results across the storage hierarchy, and lets scientists query the enriched metadata instead of post-processing the raw output.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Coeus project page](/research/projects/coeus/)"},{"id":"/2022/05/07/sun-msu-distinguished-alumni","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/05/07/sun-msu-distinguished-alumni","source":"@site/blog/2022-05-07-sun-msu-distinguished-alumni.mdx","title":"Xian-He Sun receives the MSU CSE Distinguished Alumni Award","description":"Michigan State\'s College of Engineering gave Xian-He Sun, who earned his PhD there, its 2022 Computer Science and Engineering Distinguished Alumni Award.","date":"2022-05-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"}],"readingTime":0.61,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Xian-He Sun receives the MSU CSE Distinguished Alumni Award","description":"Michigan State\'s College of Engineering gave Xian-He Sun, who earned his PhD there, its 2022 Computer Science and Engineering Distinguished Alumni Award.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DOE funds Coeus for accelerating scientific insights","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/09/01/coeus-doe-grant"},"nextItem":{"title":"ALCF announces the DLIO benchmark","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/09/16/alcf-dlio-benchmark"}},"content":"The Michigan State University College of Engineering has presented Xian-He Sun its [Computer Science and Engineering Distinguished Alumni Award](https://engineering.msu.edu/news/alumni-awards-2022), at a ceremony on May 7, 2022.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Michigan State\\n\\nSun earned his PhD at Michigan State University. His research since then has covered parallel computing, memory systems, and storage optimization.\\n\\n## Selected contributions\\n\\nSun\'s contributions include:\\n\\n- **Sun-Ni\'s Law**: A generalization of Amdahl\'s Law that better captures performance scaling in memory-bounded applications\\n- **C-AMAT Model**: A framework for analyzing memory access performance in concurrent systems\\n- **[Hermes](/research/projects/hermes/)**: A multi-tiered I/O buffering system for heterogeneous storage\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)\\n- [GRC research projects](/research/projects/)"},{"id":"/2021/09/16/alcf-dlio-benchmark","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/09/16/alcf-dlio-benchmark","source":"@site/blog/2021-09-16-alcf-dlio-benchmark.mdx","title":"ALCF announces the DLIO benchmark","description":"Argonne\'s ALCF announced DLIO, a deep learning I/O benchmark built from profiles of eight scientific applications on Theta with Illinois Tech\'s SCS Lab.","date":"2021-09-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"ai-data-science","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/ai-data-science"},{"inline":true,"label":"Media","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/media"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deep learning","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/deep-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"Benchmarking","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/benchmarking"},{"inline":true,"label":"Argonne","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/argonne"},{"inline":true,"label":"DLIO","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/dlio"}],"readingTime":1.23,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"ALCF announces the DLIO benchmark","description":"Argonne\'s ALCF announced DLIO, a deep learning I/O benchmark built from profiles of eight scientific applications on Theta with Illinois Tech\'s SCS Lab.","authors":"grc","tags":["ai-data-science","Media","Deep learning","Benchmarking","Argonne","DLIO"],"image":"/img/members/devarajan.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun receives the MSU CSE Distinguished Alumni Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2022/05/07/sun-msu-distinguished-alumni"},"nextItem":{"title":"NSF awards CSSI framework grant for ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/11/chronolog-nsf-grant"}},"content":"The Argonne Leadership Computing Facility has announced DLIO, the deep learning I/O benchmark developed by researchers at ALCF and the Scalable Computing Software (SCS) Lab at Illinois Tech. The benchmark is maintained in ALCF\'s GitHub organization (argonne-lcf/dlio_benchmark).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What DLIO does\\n\\nDLIO is designed to:\\n\\n- **Identify I/O bottlenecks** in deep learning training pipelines\\n- **Optimize data loading** strategies for large-scale training jobs\\n- **Evaluate storage system performance** under realistic AI workloads\\n- **Inform infrastructure decisions** for future systems\\n\\n## Who built it\\n\\nDLIO was developed by SCS Lab researchers Hariharan Devarajan, Anthony Kougkas, and Xian-He Sun with ALCF computer scientists Huihuo Zheng and Venkatram Vishwanath. The benchmark was built from I/O profiles of eight scientific deep learning applications on ALCF\'s Theta supercomputer, and the CCGrid 2021 paper describing it received the conference\'s best paper award. Devarajan began the work as an ALCF intern in 2020.\\n\\n## Why I/O matters for AI\\n\\nAs deep learning models grow, data loading takes a larger share of training time. DLIO helps researchers and system administrators find and remove these bottlenecks.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [ALCF news article, September 16, 2021](https://www.alcf.anl.gov/news/alcf-researchers-develop-benchmarking-tool-improve-efficiency-deep-learning-applications) ([archived copy](https://web.archive.org/web/20260124141859/https://www.alcf.anl.gov/news/alcf-researchers-develop-benchmarking-tool-improve-efficiency-deep-learning-applications))\\n- [DLIO on GitHub](https://github.com/argonne-lcf/dlio_benchmark)\\n- [The DLIO paper and its CCGrid 2021 Best Paper Award](/gnosis/articles/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper/)"},{"id":"/2021/05/11/chronolog-nsf-grant","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/11/chronolog-nsf-grant","source":"@site/blog/2021-05-11-chronolog-nsf-grant.mdx","title":"NSF awards CSSI framework grant for ChronoLog","description":"NSF award OAC-2104013 funds ChronoLog, a distributed, multi-tiered log store for activity data, built by Illinois Tech and the University of Chicago.","date":"2021-05-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logging","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/logging"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"NSF","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/nsf"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/chrono-log"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"NSF awards CSSI framework grant for ChronoLog","description":"NSF award OAC-2104013 funds ChronoLog, a distributed, multi-tiered log store for activity data, built by Illinois Tech and the University of Chicago.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Logging","Storage systems","NSF","ChronoLog"],"image":"/img/projects/chronolog/software.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"ALCF announces the DLIO benchmark","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/09/16/alcf-dlio-benchmark"},"nextItem":{"title":"DLIO wins the Best Paper Award (First Prize) at CCGrid 2021","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper"}},"content":"The National Science Foundation has awarded a collaborative CSSI Framework grant (OAC-2104013) for ChronoLog, a distributed and tiered logging system designed for exascale high-performance computing systems and large-scale data centers.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The logging problem at scale\\n\\nModern scientific workflows, IoT systems, and enterprise applications generate enormous volumes of log data. Traditional logging systems struggle to handle the velocity, volume, and variety of this data while maintaining the strict ordering guarantees that applications require.\\n\\nChronoLog addresses these challenges with four design choices:\\n\\n- **Time-based ordering** that eliminates expensive synchronization operations\\n- **Multi-tiered storage** that automatically moves data across storage hierarchies\\n- **Distributed architecture** that scales horizontally to handle massive throughput\\n- **Elastic I/O** that adapts to varying workload patterns\\n\\n## Two institutions\\n\\nThis project represents a collaboration between Illinois Institute of Technology and the University of Chicago, bringing together expertise in distributed systems, storage architectures, and high-performance computing.\\n\\n## Applications\\n\\nThe award abstract names applications in finance, weather, medicine, geoscience, cosmology, and astrophysics, all of which produce activity data that must be recorded in order and queried at scale.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [NSF award OAC-2104013](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2104013)\\n- [ChronoLog project page](/research/projects/chronolog/)"},{"id":"/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper","source":"@site/blog/2021-05-10-dlio-best-paper.mdx","title":"DLIO wins the Best Paper Award (First Prize) at CCGrid 2021","description":"DLIO, a data-centric benchmark that emulates the I/O behavior of scientific deep learning applications, won the CCGrid 2021 Best Paper Award (First Prize).","date":"2021-05-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"},{"inline":true,"label":"CCGrid","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/cc-grid"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deep learning","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/deep-learning"},{"inline":true,"label":"Argonne","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/argonne"},{"inline":true,"label":"DLIO","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/dlio"}],"readingTime":1.52,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"DLIO wins the Best Paper Award (First Prize) at CCGrid 2021","description":"DLIO, a data-centric benchmark that emulates the I/O behavior of scientific deep learning applications, won the CCGrid 2021 Best Paper Award (First Prize).","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award","CCGrid","Deep learning","Argonne","DLIO"],"image":"/img/members/devarajan.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"NSF awards CSSI framework grant for ChronoLog","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/11/chronolog-nsf-grant"},"nextItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun appointed Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/12/16/sun-endowed-chair"}},"content":"The paper \\"DLIO: A Data-Centric Benchmark for Scientific Deep Learning Applications\\" has received the Best Paper Award (First Prize) at the 2021 IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Cluster, Cloud, and Internet Computing (CCGrid 2021). The work, authored by Hariharan Devarajan, Huihuo Zheng, Anthony Kougkas, Xian-He Sun, and Venkatram Vishwanath, introduces a benchmarking framework that exposes the I/O bottlenecks hidden in deep learning training pipelines.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The problem\\n\\nDeep learning is used across scientific domains such as cosmology, particle physics, fusion, and astrophysics. Much work has gone into the computational performance of deep learning frameworks, but far less into their I/O, even though training relies on large and varied datasets and I/O is a significant bottleneck in large-scale distributed training.\\n\\n## DLIO\\n\\nThe paper studies the I/O behavior of several scientific deep learning workloads on the Theta supercomputer at the Argonne Leadership Computing Facility. From those profiles it builds DLIO, a benchmark suite that emulates the I/O behavior of modern scientific deep learning applications. Application developers and system architects can use it to find I/O bottlenecks and guide optimizations; the paper reports training times lowered by up to 6.7x.\\n\\n## The team\\n\\nThe authors are Hariharan Devarajan (then a PhD student in the SCS Lab, now at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory), Huihuo Zheng and Venkatram Vishwanath of Argonne National Laboratory, Anthony Kougkas, and Xian-He Sun. The work grew out of a collaboration with the Argonne Leadership Computing Facility.\\n\\n## Continued development\\n\\nThe MLPerf Storage benchmark from MLCommons is built on DLIO, and development continues in the [argonne-lcf/dlio_benchmark](https://github.com/argonne-lcf/dlio_benchmark) repository.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Read the paper](/gnosis/publications/devarajan-2021-dlio-0272/)\\n- [ALCF\'s announcement of the DLIO benchmark](/gnosis/articles/2021/09/16/alcf-dlio-benchmark/)\\n- [Hariharan Devarajan\'s profile](/center/team/hariharan-devarajan/)"},{"id":"/2020/12/16/sun-endowed-chair","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/12/16/sun-endowed-chair","source":"@site/blog/2020-12-16-sun-endowed-chair.mdx","title":"Xian-He Sun appointed Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair","description":"Illinois Tech named Xian-He Sun, Distinguished Professor of Computer Science, the Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair in Computer Science in December 2020.","date":"2020-12-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"}],"readingTime":0.77,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Xian-He Sun appointed Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair","description":"Illinois Tech named Xian-He Sun, Distinguished Professor of Computer Science, the Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair in Computer Science in December 2020.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DLIO wins the Best Paper Award (First Prize) at CCGrid 2021","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2021/05/10/dlio-best-paper"},"nextItem":{"title":"ACA 2020 keynote: data flow under the von Neumann architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/08/13/sun-aca-keynote"}},"content":"Illinois Tech has appointed Xian-He Sun, Distinguished Professor of Computer Science, as the Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair in Computer Science, as [announced on December 16, 2020](https://www.iit.edu/news/xian-he-sun-appointed-ron-hochsprung-endowed-chair-computer-science). The chair is named for Ron Hochsprung, an Illinois Tech alumnus.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The announcement\\n\\nProvost Peter Kilpatrick described Sun as \\"one of the national leaders in high-performance computing\\" who \\"has done seminal work on the scalability of parallel processor computers.\\"\\n\\nThe announcement notes that, as director of Illinois Tech\'s Scalable Computing Software Laboratory, Sun co-developed Sun-Ni\'s law, one of the three scalable computing laws alongside Amdahl\'s law and Gustafson\'s law, and helped develop C-AMAT, a model that addresses the memory-wall problem.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Illinois Tech news announcement](https://www.iit.edu/news/xian-he-sun-appointed-ron-hochsprung-endowed-chair-computer-science)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2020/08/13/sun-aca-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/08/13/sun-aca-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2020-08-13-sun-aca-keynote.mdx","title":"ACA 2020 keynote: data flow under the von Neumann architecture","description":"At the online CCF ACA 2020 conference, Xian-He Sun reexamined the von Neumann architecture from a data-centric view and introduced the dataflowV approach.","date":"2020-08-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"ACA","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/aca"},{"inline":true,"label":"Computer architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/computer-architecture"},{"inline":true,"label":"Memory systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/memory-systems"}],"readingTime":2.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"ACA 2020 keynote: data flow under the von Neumann architecture","description":"At the online CCF ACA 2020 conference, Xian-He Sun reexamined the von Neumann architecture from a data-centric view and introduced the dataflowV approach.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","ACA","Computer architecture","Memory systems"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun appointed Ron Hochsprung Endowed Chair","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/12/16/sun-endowed-chair"},"nextItem":{"title":"HPBD&IS 2020 keynote: label-based data representation for big data I/O","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/05/23/sun-hpbdis-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave an invited keynote at the 13th CCF National Computer Architecture Conference (CCF ACA 2020) on August 13, 2020. The conference, held every two years, ran online from August 10 to 16. His talk, \\"Data flow under the von Neumann Computer Architecture\\", asked how to design a data-centric architecture for the big data era and introduced an approach he named dataflowV.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## A data-centric look at von Neumann\\n\\nBig data applications have changed what computers spend their time on, and Sun opened with the question of how to design a computer architecture around data rather than computation. Both the von Neumann architecture and the classic dataflow architecture are compute-centric: dataflow machines arrange the movement of data so as to maximize parallel computation.\\n\\nLooking at von Neumann from the data side, Sun argued that the model itself is neutral between computing and memory. Its compute-centric reputation comes from history, from the decades in which processors were the scarce resource, not from the architecture.\\n\\n## Modeling memory and data movement\\n\\nThe talk then examined von Neumann memory systems from a data-centric point of view and introduced metrics and methods to model and optimize them. Sun\'s group has published several of these, including the Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (C-AMAT) model in IEEE Computer in 2014 and the Layered Performance Matching (LPM) methodology in IEEE TPDS in 2019.\\n\\nIn the talk, memory system performance is modeled with four types of memory cycles. A hierarchical analysis of those four cycle types, together with the cost of computing, gives a formal trade-off between computing and data movement.\\n\\n## DataflowV\\n\\nFrom that trade-off Sun proposed data flow under the von Neumann computer architecture, dataflowV for short. DataflowV decides where and how to compute by taking the cost of data movement into account. It is not a new computer architecture but a data-centric way of implementing the von Neumann architecture. A complete dataflowV system is a hard goal, but Sun noted that each of its point solutions can help current systems right away. He presented the concept, the modeling and the design, early implementation results, and the open research questions for dataflowV and for memory systems in general.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [ACA 2020 invited speakers, with the keynote abstract](http://tc.ccf.org.cn/tcarch/aca2020/html/speaker.html)\\n- [Concurrent Average Memory Access Time (IEEE Computer, 2014)](/gnosis/publications/sun-2014-concurrent-average-f9f1/)\\n- [LPM: A Systematic Methodology for Concurrent Data Access Pattern Optimization (IEEE TPDS, 2019)](/gnosis/publications/liu-2019-lpm-e1f4/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2020/05/23/sun-hpbdis-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/05/23/sun-hpbdis-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2020-05-23-sun-hpbdis-keynote.mdx","title":"HPBD&IS 2020 keynote: label-based data representation for big data I/O","description":"At HPBD&IS 2020, Xian-He Sun presented dLabel, a label-based data representation that separates data management from data operations, and the LABIOS system.","date":"2020-05-23T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPBD&IS","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpbd-is"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"LABIOS","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/labios"}],"readingTime":2.11,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"HPBD&IS 2020 keynote: label-based data representation for big data I/O","description":"At HPBD&IS 2020, Xian-He Sun presented dLabel, a label-based data representation that separates data management from data operations, and the LABIOS system.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","HPBD&IS","Storage systems","LABIOS"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"ACA 2020 keynote: data flow under the von Neumann architecture","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/08/13/sun-aca-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"HPC China 2019 keynote: high performance data access","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/08/22/sun-hpcchina-keynote"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave a keynote at the 2020 International Conference on High Performance Big Data and Intelligent Systems (HPBD&IS 2020) on May 23, 2020. The talk, \\"dLABEL: A Label-Based Data Representation and its implementation on Big Data I/O Systems\\", proposed attaching operations to data through labels and showed the idea working in LABIOS, GRC\'s label-based I/O system.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Conflicting demands on storage\\n\\nLarge-scale applications in the big data era have data management needs that pull in different directions. Those needs have produced a growing range of hardware devices and software stacks, many with conflicting requirements. Sun argued that, despite years of effort, data movement, storage and management are still the main performance bottlenecks of modern HPC systems.\\n\\n## Data labels\\n\\nThe talk introduced a data representation called the data label, or dLabel. A dLabel attaches an operation to the data it acts on and marks the pair with a label. Because each label carries both the data and what should happen to it, the storage system can decide where and when to carry out the operation, independently of the application that issued it. This separates data management from data operations and makes software-defined data management possible.\\n\\n## LABIOS\\n\\nTo show what dLabel can do, Sun presented LABIOS, a distributed I/O system built on labels. In the talk he reported that LABIOS improves I/O performance by up to 17 times through asynchronous I/O. It also supports heterogeneous storage resources, lets storage grow and shrink with demand, and supports in-situ analytics by provisioning data to analysis tasks. Because labels are independent units of work, one LABIOS deployment can serve I/O workloads that would conflict on a conventional file system, which helps HPC and big data workloads share the same storage.\\n\\nLABIOS was published at ACM HPDC 2019 by Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan, Jay Lofstead and Sun, where it received the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award. Sun closed by noting that LABIOS shows dLabel works on current memory and file systems, and that the full potential of the representation is still to be explored.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Keynote abstract in the HPBD&IS 2020 program (Internet Archive copy)](http://web.archive.org/web/20200530172651/http://hpbdis2020.csp.escience.cn/dct/page/65581)\\n- [LABIOS: A Distributed Label-Based I/O System (HPDC 2019)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2019-labios-8411/)\\n- [LABIOS wins the HPDC 2019 best paper award](/gnosis/articles/2019/06/24/labios-best-paper/)\\n- [LABIOS project page](/research/projects/labios/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2019/08/22/sun-hpcchina-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/08/22/sun-hpcchina-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2019-08-22-sun-hpcchina-keynote.mdx","title":"HPC China 2019 keynote: high performance data access","description":"At HPC China 2019 in Hohhot, Xian-He Sun traced the memory wall down to I/O and presented Hermes, which buffers data across a deep memory and storage hierarchy.","date":"2019-08-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPC China","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpc-china"},{"inline":true,"label":"Data buffering","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/data-buffering"},{"inline":true,"label":"HDF5","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hdf-5"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":2.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"HPC China 2019 keynote: high performance data access","description":"At HPC China 2019 in Hohhot, Xian-He Sun traced the memory wall down to I/O and presented Hermes, which buffers data across a deep memory and storage hierarchy.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","HPC China","Data buffering","HDF5","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HPBD&IS 2020 keynote: label-based data representation for big data I/O","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2020/05/23/sun-hpbdis-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"LABIOS receives the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/06/24/labios-best-paper"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave a plenary keynote, \\"High Performance Data Access: the DMSH approach\\", at the National Annual Conference on High Performance Computing (HPC China 2019) in Hohhot, China, on August 22, 2019. The talk followed the memory wall down to storage and presented Hermes, GRC\'s system for buffering data across a deep memory and storage hierarchy (DMSH).\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## From the memory wall to the I/O wall\\n\\nThe talk opened with the trend lines behind Sun\'s earlier work on memory-bounded speedup (1990) and the memory wall (1994). Processor performance grew about 52% a year until 2004, and multi-core processors have pushed aggregate performance higher since. Memory performance grew about 9% a year and I/O about 6%. The gap between processors and storage keeps widening, so I/O has become the bottleneck of HPC. Parallel I/O systems, which stripe data across many file servers, help but are not enough.\\n\\n## A deep memory and storage hierarchy\\n\\nHPC sites have begun installing new layers between memory and the parallel file system. One example in the talk was Cori, a Cray XC40 at NERSC that uses Cray\'s DataWarp burst buffer. New non-volatile memory devices and remote data access methods add further layers. Together they form a DMSH, a multi-level hierarchy in which a layer does not have to hold a copy of the data in the layer below.\\n\\nThe slides listed what was missing to use such a hierarchy well: software that manages heterogeneous tiers as a whole, intelligent placement of data among the tiers, automatic movement of data between them, native buffering support in HDF5, and the expertise that most users do not have. The stated goal was for the layers to be invisible to applications, with no loss of I/O performance.\\n\\n## Hermes\\n\\nSun\'s answer was to combine DMSH buffering with the HDF5 data format, which already carries rich information about the data it stores. Hermes, built with The HDF Group under an NSF grant, is a multi-tiered, distributed buffering platform. Its machine model has four layers: RAM, local NVMe or SSD, shared burst buffers and a remote parallel file system. Data moves vertically through the layers within a node and horizontally across nodes. Hermes components include a data placement engine that chooses a tier for each piece of data, a data organizer that carries out prefetching and eviction, a metadata manager, a cache manager with several replacement policies, and an application orchestrator that limits interference between applications sharing a tier.\\n\\nThe design drew on GRC papers from 2018: Hermes at ACM HPDC, IRIS at ACM ICS and the Harmonia I/O scheduler at IEEE Cluster, all by Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan and Sun, with Jay Lofstead on Harmonia.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Keynote slides (PDF)](http://www.cs.iit.edu/~scs/assets/files/Hermes-hpc-china.pdf)\\n- [HPC China 2019 program (Internet Archive copy)](http://web.archive.org/web/2019/http://hpcchina2019.csp.escience.cn:80/dct/page/70029)\\n- [Hermes: A Heterogeneous-Aware Multi-Tiered Distributed I/O Buffering System (HPDC 2018)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2018-hermes-25f4/)\\n- [IRIS: I/O Redirection via Integrated Storage (ICS 2018)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2018-iris-f1a6/)\\n- [Harmonia: An Interference-Aware Dynamic I/O Scheduler (Cluster 2018)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2018-harmonia-5ca9/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2019/06/24/labios-best-paper","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/06/24/labios-best-paper","source":"@site/blog/2019-06-24-labios-best-paper.mdx","title":"LABIOS receives the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019","description":"The HPDC 2019 Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award went to LABIOS, a label-based I/O system by Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan, Jay Lofstead and Xian-He Sun.","date":"2019-06-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"},{"inline":true,"label":"HPDC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hpdc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Sandia","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/sandia"},{"inline":true,"label":"LABIOS","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/labios"}],"readingTime":1.64,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"LABIOS receives the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019","description":"The HPDC 2019 Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award went to LABIOS, a label-based I/O system by Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan, Jay Lofstead and Xian-He Sun.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award","HPDC","Storage systems","Sandia","LABIOS"],"image":"/img/projects/labios/as_Storage.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"HPC China 2019 keynote: high performance data access","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/08/22/sun-hpcchina-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"ACM TURC 2019 keynote: data access of big data","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/05/19/sun-turc-keynote"}},"content":"The paper \\"LABIOS: A Distributed Label-Based I/O System\\" has received the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at the ACM 28th International Symposium on High-Performance Parallel and Distributed Computing (HPDC 2019). The award, named in honor of the late Karsten Schwan for his contributions to high-performance systems, recognizes the best paper presented at the symposium.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The LABIOS architecture\\n\\nLABIOS supports conflicting I/O workloads from scientific and Big Data applications under a single storage system. Its central idea is the label, a new data representation. A label carries a unique identifier, a pointer to the data, and the operation to perform on it, so the instruction of an I/O request travels separately from its data.\\n\\nIn LABIOS, a Label Manager builds labels from each I/O request and publishes them to a distributed label queue. A Content Manager holds the request data in a distributed in-memory warehouse, and a Catalog Manager maintains user and system metadata. A Label Dispatcher sends labels to workers using configurable scheduling policies, and a Worker Manager balances load across the worker pool and commissions or decommissions workers as needed. Because the application only enqueues labels, its I/O becomes asynchronous.\\n\\nThe paper reports that LABIOS boosts I/O performance by up to 17x in the workloads it evaluates. It also supports heterogeneous storage resources, offers storage elasticity, and enables in-situ analytics through data provisioning.\\n\\n## The team\\n\\nThe work was led by Anthony Kougkas as part of his doctoral research, with Hariharan Devarajan and Jay Lofstead (Sandia National Laboratories), under the supervision of Xian-He Sun. The label-based I/O method was later granted [US Patent 11,630,834](/gnosis/patents/label-based-io-patent/), and LABIOS continues as an active project under [NSF award OAC-2313154](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=2313154).\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Read the paper](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2019-labios-8411/)\\n- [LABIOS project page](/research/projects/labios/)\\n- [Anthony Kougkas\'s profile](/center/team/anthony-kougkas/)"},{"id":"/2019/05/19/sun-turc-keynote","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/05/19/sun-turc-keynote","source":"@site/blog/2019-05-19-sun-turc-keynote.mdx","title":"ACM TURC 2019 keynote: data access of big data","description":"At ACM TURC 2019 in Chengdu, Xian-He Sun argued that storage access is the main bottleneck of big data systems and presented the Hermes I/O buffering system.","date":"2019-05-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"knowledge-sharing","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/knowledge-sharing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Keynote","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/keynote"},{"inline":true,"label":"TURC","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/turc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage systems","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/storage-systems"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":2.1,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"ACM TURC 2019 keynote: data access of big data","description":"At ACM TURC 2019 in Chengdu, Xian-He Sun argued that storage access is the main bottleneck of big data systems and presented the Hermes I/O buffering system.","authors":"grc","tags":["knowledge-sharing","Keynote","TURC","Storage systems","Hermes"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"LABIOS receives the Karsten Schwan Best Paper Award at HPDC 2019","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/06/24/labios-best-paper"},"nextItem":{"title":"SCS Lab receives NSF framework grant for Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2018/09/12/hermes-nsf-grant"}},"content":"Xian-He Sun gave the third keynote of the SIGMOBILE symposium at the ACM Turing Celebration Conference, China (ACM TURC 2019), in Chengdu on May 19, 2019. His talk, \\"Data Access of Big Data: Solving the system bottleneck\\", made the case that storage access limits big data systems, even mobile ones, and presented Hermes as GRC\'s response.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The I/O gap\\n\\nDisk-based storage has improved far more slowly than memory and networks. The result is a wide gap between how fast a system can compute and how fast it can read and write data. Storage subsystems have answered by adopting new technologies, such as burst buffers and non-volatile memory, and by adding layers to the memory and storage hierarchy. Sun called the result a deep memory and storage hierarchy (DMSH).\\n\\nMore layers give more room for performance, but they also make data movement harder. An application now has to decide which tier should hold which data, and when to move it. Most applications cannot make those decisions well, so much of the hierarchy\'s potential goes unused.\\n\\nThe first part of the talk was about measurement: storage data access is the performance bottleneck that matters most, and that holds even in distributed mobile environments, the focus of the SIGMOBILE audience.\\n\\n## Hermes\\n\\nThe second part presented Hermes, a large NSF-supported software project that GRC leads with The HDF Group. Hermes is an intelligent, multi-tiered, dynamic and distributed I/O buffering system. It places data across the tiers of a DMSH so that applications see faster I/O without managing the tiers themselves.\\n\\nSun described two directions for the work. The first extends HPC I/O stacks so that memory and parallel I/O systems act as one integrated system. The second extends HDF5, the data format and library used widely in science, so that it can learn what an application needs and optimize data placement for it. He covered the Hermes design and implementation, what sets it apart from earlier caching layers, the open challenges, and early results. The design was first published at ACM HPDC 2018 by Anthony Kougkas, Hariharan Devarajan and Sun.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Keynote abstract in the ACM TURC 2019 SIGMOBILE program](https://www.acmturc.com/2019/en/SIGMOBILE.html)\\n- [Hermes: A Heterogeneous-Aware Multi-Tiered Distributed I/O Buffering System (HPDC 2018)](/gnosis/publications/kougkas-2018-hermes-25f4/)\\n- [NSF framework grant for Hermes](/gnosis/articles/2018/09/12/hermes-nsf-grant/)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)"},{"id":"/2018/09/12/hermes-nsf-grant","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2018/09/12/hermes-nsf-grant","source":"@site/blog/2018-09-12-hermes-nsf-grant.mdx","title":"SCS Lab receives NSF framework grant for Hermes","description":"NSF award OAC-1835764 funded Hermes, a heterogeneous-aware, multi-tiered distributed I/O buffering system that extends HDF5 to deep storage hierarchies.","date":"2018-09-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"funding-win","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/funding-win"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grant","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/grant"},{"inline":true,"label":"Data buffering","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/data-buffering"},{"inline":true,"label":"HDF5","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hdf-5"},{"inline":true,"label":"NSF","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/nsf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/hermes"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"SCS Lab receives NSF framework grant for Hermes","description":"NSF award OAC-1835764 funded Hermes, a heterogeneous-aware, multi-tiered distributed I/O buffering system that extends HDF5 to deep storage hierarchies.","authors":"grc","tags":["funding-win","Grant","Data buffering","HDF5","NSF","Hermes"],"image":"/img/projects/hermes/architecture.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"ACM TURC 2019 keynote: data access of big data","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2019/05/19/sun-turc-keynote"},"nextItem":{"title":"Xian-He Sun elected IEEE Fellow","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2012/01/05/sun-ieee-fellow"}},"content":"The National Science Foundation has awarded a Framework Grant (OAC-1835764) to Xian-He Sun for the Hermes project, a heterogeneous-aware multi-tiered distributed I/O buffering system designed to address the growing performance gap between computing power and storage systems.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The Hermes design\\n\\nComputing power on supercomputers has grown much faster than storage performance, a gap researchers call the \\"storage wall.\\" Hermes addresses it by managing data across multiple storage tiers, from memory to disk.\\n\\nThe project extends the widely used HDF Library to support intelligent I/O buffering for deep memory and storage hierarchy systems, making high-performance I/O accessible to the broader scientific computing community. It is a collaborative project with The HDF Group: the award lists Gerd Heber, Ann Johnson and Elena Pourmal of The HDF Group as co-PIs.\\n\\n## Why it matters\\n\\nScientific applications write and read large datasets through storage hierarchies that now include memory, node-local NVMe, burst buffers, and parallel file systems. Hermes buffers I/O across those tiers so that applications spend less time waiting for data.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [NSF award OAC-1835764](https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/show-award/?AWD_ID=1835764)\\n- [Hermes project page](/research/projects/hermes/)"},{"id":"/2012/01/05/sun-ieee-fellow","metadata":{"permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2012/01/05/sun-ieee-fellow","source":"@site/blog/2012-01-05-sun-ieee-fellow.mdx","title":"Xian-He Sun elected IEEE Fellow","description":"IEEE elevated Xian-He Sun to Fellow in its class of 2011 for contributions to memory-bounded performance metrics and scalable parallel computing.","date":"2012-01-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"awards-glory","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/awards-glory"},{"inline":true,"label":"Award","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/tags/award"}],"readingTime":0.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Gnosis Research Center","url":"https://grc.iit.edu","imageURL":"/img/logo.png","key":"grc","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Xian-He Sun elected IEEE Fellow","description":"IEEE elevated Xian-He Sun to Fellow in its class of 2011 for contributions to memory-bounded performance metrics and scalable parallel computing.","authors":"grc","tags":["awards-glory","Award"],"image":"/img/members/sun.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SCS Lab receives NSF framework grant for Hermes","permalink":"/gnosis/articles/2018/09/12/hermes-nsf-grant"}},"content":"The IEEE has elected Xian-He Sun, chair of the Department of Computer Science at Illinois Tech, to its class of 2011 Fellows \\"for contributions to memory-bounded performance metrics and scalable parallel computing,\\" as [Illinois Tech announced on January 5, 2012](https://www.iit.edu/news/computer-science-chair-xian-he-sun-elected-ieee-fellow). Fellow is the IEEE\'s highest grade of membership, held by approximately 0.1 percent of its voting members.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The citation\\n\\nThe citation refers to the memory-bounded speedup model, known as Sun-Ni\'s Law, which Sun and Lionel Ni introduced in 1990. Amdahl\'s Law fixes the problem size and Gustafson\'s Law fixes the execution time; the memory-bounded model instead scales the problem with the memory available as processors are added, so that memory capacity enters the analysis of parallel scalability. Sun\'s later work on concurrent memory access, including the C-AMAT model published in IEEE Computer in 2014, continued this line of research in the SCS Lab, the predecessor of the Gnosis Research Center.\\n\\n## Learn more\\n\\n- [Xian-He Sun\'s profile](/center/team/xian-he-sun/)\\n- [Sun and Ni, \\"Another View on Parallel Speedup\\" (Supercomputing 1990)](/gnosis/publications/sun-1990-another-view-b7a2/)"}]}}')}}]);